window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-05"]=[{"id":"q2-83","q":"2:83","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:83 · The covenant of worship and beneficence","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 127–128","cairo":"I:127–128","source_page_first":127,"source_page_last":128,"ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ لا تَعْبُدُونَ إِلاَّ اللَّهَ وَبِالْوالِدَيْنِ إِحْساناً وَذِي الْقُرْبى وَالْيَتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ وَقُولُوا لِلنَّاسِ حُسْناً وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكاةَ ثُمَّ تَوَلَّيْتُمْ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً مِنْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ مُعْرِضُونَ (83)\nقلت: (لا تعبدون) : خبر في معنى النهي، كقوله تعالى: وَلا يُضَارَّ كاتِبٌ وَلا شَهِيدٌ، وهو أبلغ من صريح النهي، لما فيه من إيهام أن المنهي سارع إلى الانتهاء، وقيل: حُذفت «أن» ، وارتفع المضارع، وهو على حذف القول، أي: وقلنا لهم: لا تعبدون، وقرأ ابن كثير وحمزة والكسائي بالغيب.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: واذكروا إذ أخذنا الميثاق على بني إسرائيل وقلنا لهم: لا يتصوّر منكم شرك معي ولا ميل إلى غيري، فلا تعبدوا إلا إياي، وأحسنوا بِالْوالِدَيْنِ إحسانا كاملا، وأحسنوا بِذِي الْقُرْبى نسباً وديناً، وأحسنوا باليتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ، بالمواساة والملاطفة، وَقُولُوا لِلنَّاسِ قولاً حُسْناً أو ذا حسن، وهو ما لا لغو فيه، ولا تأثيم بل ما فيه نصح وإرشاد، وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلاةَ بإتقان شروطها وكمال آدابها، وأدوا الزَّكاةَ لمستحقها، ثُمَّ بعد ذلك تَوَلَّيْتُمْ، وأعرضتم إِلَّا قَلِيلًا ممن أسلم مِنْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ مُعْرِضُونَ عن الحق بعد ظهوره.\nذكر الحق تعالى في هذا العهد أربعة أعمال: عمل خاص بالقلب، وهو التوحيد، وعمل خاص بالبدن، وهو الصلاة، وعمل خاص بالمال، وهو الزكاة، وعمل عام وهو الإحسان، ورتَّبها باعتبار الأهم فالأهم، فقدّم الوالدين لتأكيد حقهما الأعظم، ثم القرابة لأن فيهم أجر الإحسان وصلة الرحم، ثم اليتامى لقلّة حيلتهم، ثم المساكين لضعفهم، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: كل عهد أخذ على بني إسرائيل يؤخذ مثله على الأمة المحمدية، وهذا حكمة ذكر قصصهم لنا، وسرد مساوئهم علينا لنتحرز من الوقوع فيما وقعوا فيه، فنهلك كما هلكوا، وكل عهد أخذ على العموم باعتبار\nالظاهر يؤخذ مثله على الخصوص باعتبار الباطن، فقد أخذ الحق سبحانه العهد على المتوجهين إليه ألا تتوجه همتهم إلا إليه، ولا يعتمدون بقلوبهم إلا عليه، وأن يتخلقوا بالإحسان، مع الأقارب والأجانب وكافة الإخوان، وخصوصاً الوالدين من قِبل البشرية أو الروحانية، وهم أهل التربية النبوية، فحقوق أب الروحانية تُقدم على أب البشرية، لأنَّ أب البشرية كان سبباً في خروجه إلى دار الفناء والهوان، وأب الروحانية كان سببًا في دخوله إلى رَوْحٍ وريحان.\nوأخذ العهد على المتوجهين أن يكلموا الناس بالملاطفة والإحسان، ويرشدوهم إلى الكريم المنان، ويقيموا الصلاة بالجوارح والقلوب، ويؤدوا زكاة نفوسهم بتطهيرها من العيوب، فمن تولّى بعد ذلك فأولئك هم الفاسقون، وعن دائرة الولاية خارجون.\nثم وبّخهم على نقض عهد آخر، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَ بَنِي إِسْرائِيلَ لا تَعْبُدُونَ إِلاَّ اللَّهَ وَبِالْوالِدَيْنِ إِحْساناً وَذِي الْقُرْبى وَالْيَتامى وَالْمَساكِينِ وَقُولُوا لِلنَّاسِ حُسْناً وَأَقِيمُوا الصَّلاةَ وَآتُوا الزَّكاةَ ثُمَّ تَوَلَّيْتُمْ إِلاَّ قَلِيلاً مِنْكُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ مُعْرِضُونَ (83)","quran_en":"83. When We took the covenant of the Children of Israel: “Worship none but God; show kindness to parents, kindred, orphans and the needy; speak kindly to people; establish prayer and give the purifying alms.” Then you turned away, except a few of you, and remained averse.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"A prohibition expressed as a statement","html":"<p>I say: “You do not worship” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا تعبدون<\/bdi>) is a declarative expression with prohibitive meaning, like “No scribe or witness shall be harmed” [2:282]. It is more emphatic than an explicit prohibition, suggesting that those forbidden have already hastened to desist. Alternatively, <i>an<\/i> is omitted and the imperfect consequently nominative. A verb of saying is also omitted: “We told them, ‘You do not worship…’” Ibn Kathīr, Ḥamza and al-Kisāʾī read the verb in the third person.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “You do not worship” ( لا تعبدون ) is a declarative expression with prohibitive meaning, like “No scribe or witness shall be harmed” [2:282]. It is more emphatic than an explicit prohibition, suggesting that those forbidden have already hastened to desist. Alternatively, an is omitted and the imperfect consequently nominative. A verb of saying is also omitted: “We told them, ‘You do not worship…’” Ibn Kathīr, Ḥamza and al-Kisāʾī read the verb in the third person."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Four forms of obedience and the order of their claims","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when We bound the Children of Israel by covenant and told them: association of another with Me or inclination to another should not be conceivable from you, so worship Me alone. Show complete goodness to parents, and goodness to relatives by lineage and religion. Treat orphans and the needy with generosity and gentleness. Speak good words to people, free of empty talk and sin, containing sincere counsel and direction. Establish prayer with its conditions perfected and its courtesies complete, and give the alms to those entitled. Yet you subsequently turned away, except the few among you who embraced Islam, averting yourselves from truth after its manifestation.<\/p><p>The Real mentions four works in this covenant: one proper to the heart, affirming divine unity; one to the body, prayer; one to wealth, almsgiving; and one comprehensive, beneficence. He orders its recipients by priority: parents first because of their greatest and most binding right; then relatives, since kindness to them unites the reward of beneficence and maintaining kinship; then orphans because of their scant resources; then the needy because of their weakness. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when We bound the Children of Israel by covenant and told them: association of another with Me or inclination to another should not be conceivable from you, so worship Me alone. Show complete goodness to parents, and goodness to relatives by lineage and religion. Treat orphans and the needy with generosity and gentleness. Speak good words to people, free of empty talk and sin, containing sincere counsel and direction. Establish prayer with its conditions perfected and its courtesies complete, and give the alms to those entitled. Yet you subsequently turned away, except the few among you who embraced Islam, averting yourselves from truth after its manifestation. The Real mentions four works in this covenant: one proper to the heart, affirming divine unity; one to the body, prayer; one to wealth, almsgiving; and one comprehensive, beneficence. He orders its recipients by priority: parents first because of their greatest and most binding right; then relatives, since kindness to them unites the reward of beneficence and maintaining kinship; then orphans because of their scant resources; then the needy because of their weakness. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The inward covenant and spiritual parenthood","html":"<p>A covenant like every covenant taken from the Children of Israel is taken from the Muḥammadan community. This is the wisdom of recounting their stories and faults: we should guard against falling into what destroyed them. Corresponding to each outward covenant taken from the generality,<\/p><p>an inward covenant is taken from the elect. The Real binds those turning towards Him to direct their aspiration only to Him and rely in their hearts only upon Him; to adopt beneficence towards relatives, strangers and all the brethren, especially parents, whether of bodily or spiritual parenthood. The latter are those who practise Prophetic formation. The rights of the spiritual father take precedence over those of the bodily father: bodily fatherhood occasioned entry into the abode of passing-away and humiliation, whereas spiritual fatherhood occasioned entry into repose and fragrance.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-60\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B056]<\/a><\/p><p>Those turning towards Him are bound to speak gently and kindly, guide people to the Generous Bestower, establish prayer through limbs and hearts, and give their souls’ alms by purifying them of faults. Whoever turns away after this is a transgressor, outside the circle of friendship with God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"A covenant like every covenant taken from the Children of Israel is taken from the Muḥammadan community. This is the wisdom of recounting their stories and faults: we should guard against falling into what destroyed them. Corresponding to each outward covenant taken from the generality, an inward covenant is taken from the elect. The Real binds those turning towards Him to direct their aspiration only to Him and rely in their hearts only upon Him; to adopt beneficence towards relatives, strangers and all the brethren, especially parents, whether of bodily or spiritual parenthood. The latter are those who practise Prophetic formation. The rights of the spiritual father take precedence over those of the bodily father: bodily fatherhood occasioned entry into the abode of passing-away and humiliation, whereas spiritual fatherhood occasioned entry into repose and fragrance. [B056] Those turning towards Him are bound to speak gently and kindly, guide people to the Generous Bestower, establish prayer through limbs and hearts, and give their souls’ alms by purifying them of faults. Whoever turns away after this is a transgressor, outside the circle of friendship with God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He reproaches their violation of another covenant, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He reproaches their violation of another covenant, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C127.1","page":127,"ar":"في الأصول: فتهلكوا.","en":"The source copies read: “so that you perish” (fa-tahlikū).","anchor":"فنهلك كما هلكوا"}],"english_characters":2935,"arabic_characters":2306},{"id":"q2-84-85","q":"2:84–85","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:84–85 · Killing, expulsion and selective fidelity to Scripture","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 128–130","cairo":"I:128–130","source_page_first":128,"source_page_last":130,"ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ لا تَسْفِكُونَ دِماءَكُمْ وَلا تُخْرِجُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِكُمْ ثُمَّ أَقْرَرْتُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ تَشْهَدُونَ (84) ثُمَّ أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ تَقْتُلُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ وَتُخْرِجُونَ فَرِيقاً مِنْكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ تَظاهَرُونَ عَلَيْهِمْ بِالْإِثْمِ وَالْعُدْوانِ وَإِنْ يَأْتُوكُمْ أَسْرَى تفدوهم وَهُوَ مُحَرَّمٌ عَلَيْكُمْ إِخْراجُهُمْ أَفَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِبَعْضِ الْكِتابِ وَتَكْفُرُونَ بِبَعْضٍ فَما جَزاءُ مَنْ يَفْعَلُ ذلِكَ مِنْكُمْ إِلاَّ خِزْيٌ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا وَيَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ يُرَدُّونَ إِلى أَشَدِّ الْعَذابِ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (85)\nقلت: (ثُمَّ أَنتُمْ هَؤُلاء) «أنتم» : مبتدأ، و «هؤلاء» : خبر، و «تَقْتُلُون» : حال، كقولك: أنت ذلك الرجل الذي فعلت كذا وكذا، أو «هَؤُلاءِ» : بدل، و «تَقْتُلُونَ» : خبر أو منادي، أي: يا هؤلاء، أو منصوب على الاختصاص، والْعُدْوَانِ: الإفراط في الظلم، و «أسارى» حال، جمع أسير، ويجمع على أًسرى، وقرئ به أي:\nمأسورين، و «هُوَ» ضمير الشأن، و «مُحَرَّمٌ» خبر، و «إِخْرَاجُهُمْ» مبتدأ مؤخر، أو ضمير الإخراج فيكون مبتدأ، و «مُحَرَّمٌ» خبره، و «إِخْرَاجُهُمْ» بدل من الضمير، وهذه الجملة متصلة بقوله: وَتُخْرِجُونَ فَرِيقاً مِنْكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ تَظاهَرُونَ عَلَيْهِمْ بِالْإِثْمِ وَالْعُدْوانِ، وما قبلها اعتراض.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاذكروا أيضا إِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ وقلنا لكم لا تَسْفِكُونَ دِماءَكُمْ أي: لا يسفك بعضكم دم بعض، وَلا تُخْرِجُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِكُمْ أي: لا يخرج أحدكم أخاه من داره\nويجليه عنها، وجعلهم الحق نفسا واحدة، وكذلك هو في الحقيقة، وفي ذلك يقول الشاعر:\nعُنْصُرُ الأنفاسِ مِنَّا واحِدٌ ... وكّذا الأجْسَامُ جِسْمٌ عَمَّنَا\nثُمَّ أَقْرَرْتُمْ بهذا العهد والتزمتموه لأنفسكم وَأَنْتُمْ تَشْهَدُونَ على أنفسكم بذلك، ثُمَّ أَنْتُمْ يا هؤُلاءِ اليهود تَقْتُلُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ أي: يقتل بعضكم بعضاً، وَتُخْرِجُونَ فَرِيقاً مِنْكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ إجلاءً عنها، تتغالبون عَلَيْهِمْ بالظلم والطغيان، وَإِنْ يَأْتُوكُمْ مأسورين تفدوهم بمالكم، وذلك الإخراج محرم عليكم.\nوحاصل الآية: أن الله تعالى أخذ على بني إسرائيل العهد في التوراة ألا يقتل بعضهم بعضاً، ولا يخرج بعضهم بعضاً من ديارهم، وأيّما عبدٍ أو أمَةٍ وجدتموه من بني إسرائيل أسيراً فاشتروه بما كان من ثمنه واعتقوه، فكانت قريظة حلفاء الأوس، والنضير حلفاء الخزرج، وكانوا يقتتلون في الحرب فيُعين بنو قريظة حلفاءهم الأوس، فيقاتلون بني النضير في قتالهم مع الخزرج، فإذا غلبوا خربوا ديارهم وأخرجوهم منها، فإذا أسر رجل من الفريقين جمعوا له حتى يفدوه، فعيرتهم العرب، فقالوا: تقاتلونهم وتفدونهم؟! فيقولون: قد أمرنا أن نفديهم وحُرم علينا قتالهم، قالوا: فلم تقاتلونهم؟ فقالوا: إنا نسْتحي أن يُذَل حلفاؤنا، فوبخهم الله على ذلك، فقال:\nأَفَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِبَعْضِ الْكِتابِ وهو الفداء وَتَكْفُرُونَ بِبَعْضٍ وهو القتل والإخراج؟ فَما جَزاءُ مَنْ يَفْعَلُ ذلِكَ مِنْكُمْ إِلَّا خِزْيٌ أي: ذل وهوان فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا، وهو السبي والقتل لبني قريظة، والجلاء والإخراج من الوطن لبني النضير، أو الذل والجزية للفريقين إلى يوم القيامة، وَيَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ يُرَدُّونَ إِلى أَشَدِّ الْعَذابِ. وليس ما أصابهم تكفيراً لذنوبهم، بل نقمة وغضباً عليهم، وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ.\nالإشارة: الناس على قسمين: قوم ضعفاء تمسكوا بظاهر الشريعة ولم ينفذوا إلى باطنها، ولم يقدروا على قتل نفوسهم، ولا على الخروج من وطن عوائدهم، فيقول لهم الحق جلّ جلاله: لا تسفكون دماءكم في محبتي لأنكم لا تقدرون على ذلك، ولا تخرجون أنفسكم من دياركم في سياحة قلوبكم، فقد أقررتم بعجزكم وضعفكم، ويقول للأقوياء: ثم أنتم يا هؤلاء تقتلون أنفسكم في طلب معرفتي، وتخرجون فريقاً منكم من ديار عوائدهم في طلب مرضاتي، تتعاونون على نفوسكم بالقهر والغلبة، وكذلك ورد في بعض الأخبار: (أول ما يقول الله للعبد: اطلب العافية والجنة والأعمال وغير ذلك، فإن قال: لا، ما أريد إلا أنت، قال له: من دخل في هذا معي فإنما يدخل بإسقاط الحظوظ، ورفع الحدث، وإثبات القدم، وذلك يوجب العدم) وأنشدوا:\nمنْ لَم يَكُن فَانِياً عَنْ حَظِّهِ ... وَعِنِ الفَنَا والأُنسِ بالأحْبَابِ\nفلأَنَّهُ بَيْنَ المنَازِل واقفٌ ... لِمنَالِ حَظّ أَوْ لُحُسْنِ مَآبِ\nويقول أيضاً للأقوياء الذين قتلوا أنفسهم وخرجوا عن عوائدهم: وإن يأتوكم أسارى في أيدي نفوسهم وعوائدهم، أو في طلب الدنيا وشهواتها، تفدوهم من أسرهم، وتفكوهم من قيودهم، وتدخلوهم في حضرة مولاهم، وفي بعض الآثار: (طالب الدنيا أسير، وطالب الآخرة أجير، وطالب الحق أمير) هـ. والأمير هو الذي يفك الأسارى من أيدي العدو، لأجل ما ملكه الله من القوة والاستعداد، فإذا انفك العبد من هواه، دخل في حضرة مولاه، فمن رام إخراجه منها بعد دخوله يقال له: وهو محرم عليكم إخراجهم، فكيف تؤمنون بظاهر الشريعة وتنكرون علم الطريقة، وأنوار الحقيقة؟ فما جزاء من يفعل ذلك منكم إلا خزي في الحياةِ الدنيا وهو الحرص والطمع، والخوف والجزع وطول الأمل، وعدم النهوض إلى العمل، (ويوم القيامة يردون إلى أشد العذاب) ، وهو غم الحجاب وسوء الحساب، (وما الله بغافل عما يعملون) .\nثم بين الحق تعالى وصفهم وذكر ما أعد لهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ لا تَسْفِكُونَ دِماءَكُمْ وَلا تُخْرِجُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِكُمْ ثُمَّ أَقْرَرْتُمْ وَأَنْتُمْ تَشْهَدُونَ (84) ثُمَّ أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ تَقْتُلُونَ أَنْفُسَكُمْ وَتُخْرِجُونَ فَرِيقاً مِنْكُمْ مِنْ دِيارِهِمْ تَظاهَرُونَ عَلَيْهِمْ بِالْإِثْمِ وَالْعُدْوانِ وَإِنْ يَأْتُوكُمْ أَسْرَى تفدوهم وَهُوَ مُحَرَّمٌ عَلَيْكُمْ إِخْراجُهُمْ أَفَتُؤْمِنُونَ بِبَعْضِ الْكِتابِ وَتَكْفُرُونَ بِبَعْضٍ فَما جَزاءُ مَنْ يَفْعَلُ ذلِكَ مِنْكُمْ إِلاَّ خِزْيٌ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا وَيَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ يُرَدُّونَ إِلى أَشَدِّ الْعَذابِ وَمَا اللَّهُ بِغافِلٍ عَمَّا تَعْمَلُونَ (85)","quran_en":"84. When We took your covenant: “Do not shed one another’s blood or expel one another from your homes.” You acknowledged it, yourselves bearing witness. 85. Yet here you are, killing one another and expelling a group of your own people from their homes, supporting one another against them in sin and aggression. If they come to you as captives, you ransom them, although their expulsion was forbidden to you. Do you believe in part of the Book and disbelieve in part? Those among you who do this have no recompense but disgrace in worldly life; on the Day of Resurrection they shall be returned to the severest punishment. God is not unaware of what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The demonstrative and the pronoun of the affair","html":"<p>I say: in “then you, these…” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ثم أنتم هؤلاء<\/bdi>), “you” is the subject, “these” its predicate, and “killing” a circumstantial expression, as when one says, “You are that man, having done such-and-such.” Alternatively, “these” is an apposition and “killing” the predicate; or “these” is vocative, “you people”; or accusative through specification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاختصاص<\/bdi>). Aggression (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العدوان<\/bdi>) is excess in wrongdoing.<\/p><p>“Captives” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أسارى<\/bdi>) is circumstantial, the plural of <i>asīr<\/i>, also pluralized <i>asrā<\/i>, as in another reading: “being captives.” <i>Huwa<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">هو<\/bdi>) is the pronoun of the affair (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ضمير الشأن<\/bdi>), “forbidden” a predicate, and “their expulsion” a delayed subject. Alternatively, <i>huwa<\/i> refers to expulsion and is the subject, “forbidden” its predicate, with “their expulsion” an apposition to the pronoun. This sentence connects with “you expel a group from their homes, supporting one another against them in sin and aggression”; the intervening words are a parenthesis.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: in “then you, these…” ( ثم أنتم هؤلاء ), “you” is the subject, “these” its predicate, and “killing” a circumstantial expression, as when one says, “You are that man, having done such-and-such.” Alternatively, “these” is an apposition and “killing” the predicate; or “these” is vocative, “you people”; or accusative through specification ( الاختصاص ). Aggression ( العدوان ) is excess in wrongdoing. “Captives” ( أسارى ) is circumstantial, the plural of asīr , also pluralized asrā , as in another reading: “being captives.” Huwa ( هو ) is the pronoun of the affair ( ضمير الشأن ), “forbidden” a predicate, and “their expulsion” a delayed subject. Alternatively, huwa refers to expulsion and is the subject, “forbidden” its predicate, with “their expulsion” an apposition to the pronoun. This sentence connects with “you expel a group from their homes, supporting one another against them in sin and aggression”; the intervening words are a parenthesis."},{"type":"exposition","title":"A people treated as one soul","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when We bound you by covenant and told you not to shed your blood—that is, not to shed one another’s blood—or expel yourselves from your homes: no one should drive his brother out of his dwelling<\/p><p>and banish him. The Real makes them one soul, which in reality they are. As the poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The source of our breaths is one;<br>our bodies too are one body encompassing us.<\/blockquote><p>You acknowledged this covenant, committed yourselves to it and bore witness against yourselves. Yet you Jewish people kill one another and expel a group from their homes, prevailing over them through injustice and tyranny. If they come to you as captives, you ransom them with your wealth, though that expulsion is forbidden to you.<\/p><p>The verse’s substance is that God bound the Children of Israel in the Torah not to kill or expel one another, and to purchase and free any Israelite man or woman found enslaved, whatever the price. Qurayẓa were allies of the Aws, and al-Naḍīr of the Khazraj. When war broke out, Qurayẓa supported the Aws against al-Naḍīr fighting with the Khazraj. The victors destroyed their opponents’ homes and expelled them. If a man from either side was captured, they collected money to ransom him. The Arabs reproached them: “You fight them and then ransom them?” They replied, “We are commanded to ransom them and forbidden to fight them.” “Why, then, fight?” “We are ashamed to see our allies humiliated.” God rebuked them: do you believe in the portion of the Book commanding ransom and disbelieve in the portion forbidding killing and expulsion?<\/p><p>Those who do this have no recompense but disgrace—humiliation and abasement—in worldly life: captivity and killing for Qurayẓa, exile from their homeland for al-Naḍīr; or abasement and tribute for both until the Day of Resurrection. Then they are returned to the severest punishment. What befell them is not expiation of sins, but vengeance and wrath. God is not unaware of what you do.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember when We bound you by covenant and told you not to shed your blood—that is, not to shed one another’s blood—or expel yourselves from your homes: no one should drive his brother out of his dwelling and banish him. The Real makes them one soul, which in reality they are. As the poet says: The source of our breaths is one; our bodies too are one body encompassing us. You acknowledged this covenant, committed yourselves to it and bore witness against yourselves. Yet you Jewish people kill one another and expel a group from their homes, prevailing over them through injustice and tyranny. If they come to you as captives, you ransom them with your wealth, though that expulsion is forbidden to you. The verse’s substance is that God bound the Children of Israel in the Torah not to kill or expel one another, and to purchase and free any Israelite man or woman found enslaved, whatever the price. Qurayẓa were allies of the Aws, and al-Naḍīr of the Khazraj. When war broke out, Qurayẓa supported the Aws against al-Naḍīr fighting with the Khazraj. The victors destroyed their opponents’ homes and expelled them. If a man from either side was captured, they collected money to ransom him. The Arabs reproached them: “You fight them and then ransom them?” They replied, “We are commanded to ransom them and forbidden to fight them.” “Why, then, fight?” “We are ashamed to see our allies humiliated.” God rebuked them: do you believe in the portion of the Book commanding ransom and disbelieve in the portion forbidding killing and expulsion? Those who do this have no recompense but disgrace—humiliation and abasement—in worldly life: captivity and killing for Qurayẓa, exile from their homeland for al-Naḍīr; or abasement and tribute for both until the Day of Resurrection. Then they are returned to the severest punishment. What befell them is not expiation of sins, but vengeance and wrath. God is not unaware of what you do."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Different capacities and freeing the captives of desire","html":"<p>People are of two kinds. The weak hold to the Sacred Law’s outward aspect without penetrating its inward reality. They cannot put their lower selves to death or leave the homeland of their habits. The Real tells them: do not shed your blood in My love, for you cannot bear it; do not leave your homes through the journeying of your hearts, since you have acknowledged your weakness and inability.<\/p><p>He tells the strong: you people put your lower selves to death in seeking My knowledge and bring a group of you out of their accustomed homes in seeking My good pleasure, helping one another overcome your lower selves. Thus one report says: “God first tells the servant to seek well-being, Paradise, works and other things. If he says, ‘No, I desire only You,’ He tells him: whoever enters this with Me enters only by relinquishing allotted shares, removing the originated and affirming the eternal (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">رفع الحدث وإثبات القدم<\/bdi>); that entails non-being.” The following is recited:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">One not effaced from his own share,<br>from effacement itself and intimacy with beloveds,<br>remains standing between the stations,<br>seeking a share or a beautiful destination.<\/blockquote><p>He also tells the strong who have overcome their lower selves and left their habits: if others come captive in the hands of their lower selves and habits, or in pursuit of the world and its desires, ransom them from captivity, free their bonds and admit them to their Master’s Presence. In one report: “The seeker of the world is a captive; the seeker of the Hereafter a hired worker; the seeker of the Real a prince.” A prince frees captives from the enemy’s hands through the strength and preparedness God grants him. When the servant is freed from caprice, he enters his Master’s Presence. Anyone seeking to expel him after entry is told: their expulsion is forbidden to you.<\/p><p>How can you believe in the Sacred Law’s outward aspect while denying the science of the Path and the lights of Reality? Whoever does this receives disgrace in worldly life: greed, covetousness, fear, anxiety, long hopes and failure to rise to action. On the Day of Resurrection they return to the severest punishment: the gloom of the veil and an evil reckoning. God is not unaware of what they do.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"People are of two kinds. The weak hold to the Sacred Law’s outward aspect without penetrating its inward reality. They cannot put their lower selves to death or leave the homeland of their habits. The Real tells them: do not shed your blood in My love, for you cannot bear it; do not leave your homes through the journeying of your hearts, since you have acknowledged your weakness and inability. He tells the strong: you people put your lower selves to death in seeking My knowledge and bring a group of you out of their accustomed homes in seeking My good pleasure, helping one another overcome your lower selves. Thus one report says: “God first tells the servant to seek well-being, Paradise, works and other things. If he says, ‘No, I desire only You,’ He tells him: whoever enters this with Me enters only by relinquishing allotted shares, removing the originated and affirming the eternal ( رفع الحدث وإثبات القدم ); that entails non-being.” The following is recited: One not effaced from his own share, from effacement itself and intimacy with beloveds, remains standing between the stations, seeking a share or a beautiful destination. He also tells the strong who have overcome their lower selves and left their habits: if others come captive in the hands of their lower selves and habits, or in pursuit of the world and its desires, ransom them from captivity, free their bonds and admit them to their Master’s Presence. In one report: “The seeker of the world is a captive; the seeker of the Hereafter a hired worker; the seeker of the Real a prince.” A prince frees captives from the enemy’s hands through the strength and preparedness God grants him. When the servant is freed from caprice, he enters his Master’s Presence. Anyone seeking to expel him after entry is told: their expulsion is forbidden to you. How can you believe in the Sacred Law’s outward aspect while denying the science of the Path and the lights of Reality? Whoever does this receives disgrace in worldly life: greed, covetousness, fear, anxiety, long hopes and failure to rise to action. On the Day of Resurrection they return to the severest punishment: the gloom of the veil and an evil reckoning. God is not unaware of what they do."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High states their character and what He has prepared for them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High states their character and what He has prepared for them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C130.1","page":130,"ar":"نسبهما الطوسي في اللمع لأبي علي الروذباري.","en":"Al-Ṭūsī, in al-Lumaʿ, attributes the two lines to Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī.","anchor":"من لم يكن فانيا عن حظه"}],"english_characters":5241,"arabic_characters":4527},{"id":"q2-86","q":"2:86","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:86 · The exchange of the lasting for the passing","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 130–130","cairo":"I:130","source_page_first":130,"source_page_last":130,"ar":"أُولئِكَ الَّذِينَ اشْتَرَوُا الْحَياةَ الدُّنْيا بِالْآخِرَةِ فَلا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ الْعَذابُ وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ (86)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أُولئِكَ الناقضون للعهود المتعدون الحدود اشْتَرَوُا الْحَياةَ الدُّنْيا وزخارفها الغرارة بِالْآخِرَةِ الباقية الدائمة، فَلا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ الْعَذابُ ساعة فى الدنيا بالذل والهوان، وفي الآخرة بدخول النيران، وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ بالامتناع منه في كل أوان.\nالإشارة: أولئك الذين نظروا إلى غرة ظاهر الأكوان، ولم ينفذوا إلى عبرة باطنها، فلا ينقطع عنهم عذاب الوهم والحجاب، ولا هم يُنصرون من أليم العذاب.\nثم وبّخهم الحق تعالى على تكذيب الرسل وقتلهم إياهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"أُولئِكَ الَّذِينَ اشْتَرَوُا الْحَياةَ الدُّنْيا بِالْآخِرَةِ فَلا يُخَفَّفُ عَنْهُمُ الْعَذابُ وَلا هُمْ يُنْصَرُونَ (86)","quran_en":"86. These are those who purchase worldly life at the price of the Hereafter. Their punishment shall not be lightened, nor shall they be helped.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The price of a broken covenant","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: these covenant-breakers and transgressors of the limits have bought worldly life and its deceptive adornments at the price of the enduring, everlasting Hereafter. Their punishment is not lightened for an hour: humiliation and abasement in this world, entry into the fires in the next. Nor are they helped to ward it off at any time.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: these covenant-breakers and transgressors of the limits have bought worldly life and its deceptive adornments at the price of the enduring, everlasting Hereafter. Their punishment is not lightened for an hour: humiliation and abasement in this world, entry into the fires in the next. Nor are they helped to ward it off at any time."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The outward lure and the inward lesson","html":"<p>These looked at the deceptive outward appearance of created things without penetrating their inward lesson. The torment of illusion and the veil therefore does not cease, nor are they rescued from painful punishment.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"These looked at the deceptive outward appearance of created things without penetrating their inward lesson. The torment of illusion and the veil therefore does not cease, nor are they rescued from painful punishment."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High rebukes their denying and killing the messengers, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High rebukes their denying and killing the messengers, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":665,"arabic_characters":601},{"id":"q2-87","q":"2:87","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:87 · Messengers, the Holy Spirit and opposition to the divine call","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 130–131","cairo":"I:130–131","source_page_first":130,"source_page_last":131,"ar":"وَلَقَدْ آتَيْنا مُوسَى الْكِتابَ وَقَفَّيْنا مِنْ بَعْدِهِ بِالرُّسُلِ وَآتَيْنا عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ الْبَيِّناتِ وَأَيَّدْناهُ بِرُوحِ الْقُدُسِ أَفَكُلَّما جاءَكُمْ رَسُولٌ بِما لا تَهْوى أَنْفُسُكُمُ اسْتَكْبَرْتُمْ فَفَرِيقاً كَذَّبْتُمْ وَفَرِيقاً تَقْتُلُونَ (87)\nقلت: (قفينا) : أتبعنا، و (عيسى) عجمي معدول عن أيشوع في لغة السريانية، وهو غير منصرف للعلمية والعجمة، و (مريم) : بمعنى الخادم، ووزنه: مَفْعَل لا فعيل، و (أيدناه) أي: قويناه ونصرناه، و (روح القدس) هنا جبريل عليه السلام أي: الروح المقدسة- من إضافة الموصوف إلى الصفة-، سمي به لطهارته من كدر الحس.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَقَدْ آتَيْنا مُوسَى التوراة، فما قمتم بحقها ولا عملتم بما فيها، واتبعنا بعده الرسل كلما مات رسول بعثنا بعده آخر اعتناء بكم، وَآتَيْنا عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ المعجزات الواضحات كإحياء الموتى، وإبراء الأكمه والأبرص، والإخبار بالمغيبات، والإنجيل، وَأَيَّدْناهُ بجبريل عليه السلام كان يسير معه حيث سار، ورفعه إلى السماء حين أردتم يا معشر اليهود قتله، أَفَكُلَّما جاءَكُمْ رَسُولٌ بِما لا تَهْوى أَنْفُسُكُمُ من مشاق الطاعات وترك الحظوظ والشهوات، اسْتَكْبَرْتُمْ وامتنعتم من الإيمان به فَفَرِيقاً منهم كذبتموه كعيسى وسليمان ومحمد- عليهم السلام-، وَفَرِيقاً تقتلونه كزكريا ويحيى- عليهما السلام-؟ قال القشيري:\nأصْغَوْا إلى الداعين بسمع الهوى، فصار معبودهم صفاتهم وهواهم. هـ-.\nالإشارة: كل ما قاله الحقّ جلّ جلاله لبني إسرائيل في فحوى الخطاب يقوله لهذه الأمة في سرّ الخطاب، فلقد آتانا الكتاب، وبيَّن فيه الرشد والصواب، وقفَّى بعد إنزاله بعلماء أتقياء، وأولياء أصفياء، يحكمون بحكمه، ويهدون بهديه، فإذا أمروا بالزهد في الدنيا وترك الحظوظ والهوى رفضوهم وكذبوهم، وربما كفَّروهم وقتلوهم، واستكبروا عن الأذعان لهم والانقياد لقولهم، ففريقاً كذبوا وفريقاً يقتلون.\nوفى الحديث قال صلّى الله عليه وسلّم: «لَتَتبعُنَّ سَنَنَ مَنْ قَبْلكم شِبْراً بشِبرٍ وذَراعاً بذراعٍ، حَتَّى لَوْ دَخلُوا جُحْر ضَبٍّ لدَخَلْتُموه، فقالوا: مَن يا رسول الله اليهودُ والنصارى؟ قالَ: نعم.. ومَنْ إذن؟» أي: ومَن تتبعون إلا هم؟. فالدعاة إلى الله لا ينقطعون ما دام الدين قائماً، فقوم يدعون إلى أحكام الله، وقوم يدعون إلى معرفة الله، فالأول: العلماء، والثاني:\nالأولياء، فإذا أمروا بالخروج عن العوائد والشهوات، رموهم بسهام العتاب والمخالفات، إذ لم يأت أحد بمثل ما جاءوا به إلا عودي، إلا من خصته سابق العناية، وهبت عليه ريح الهداية، فيتبع آثارهم، وقليلٌ ما هم.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى مقالتهم الشنيعة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَقَدْ آتَيْنا مُوسَى الْكِتابَ وَقَفَّيْنا مِنْ بَعْدِهِ بِالرُّسُلِ وَآتَيْنا عِيسَى ابْنَ مَرْيَمَ الْبَيِّناتِ وَأَيَّدْناهُ بِرُوحِ الْقُدُسِ أَفَكُلَّما جاءَكُمْ رَسُولٌ بِما لا تَهْوى أَنْفُسُكُمُ اسْتَكْبَرْتُمْ فَفَرِيقاً كَذَّبْتُمْ وَفَرِيقاً تَقْتُلُونَ (87)","quran_en":"87. We gave Moses the Book and sent messengers after him in succession. We gave Jesus son of Mary clear proofs and strengthened him with the Holy Spirit. Whenever a messenger came to you with what your souls did not desire, did you grow proud—denying some and killing others?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Jesus, Mary and the Holy Spirit","html":"<p>I say: “We sent after” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قفّينا<\/bdi>) means We caused to follow. <i>ʿĪsā<\/i> is a non-Arabic name adapted from Syriac Īshūʿ, declining without full inflection because it is a foreign proper name. <i>Maryam<\/i> means a servant, and its pattern is <i>mafʿal<\/i>, not <i>faʿīl<\/i>. “We strengthened him” means We fortified and supported him. The Holy Spirit (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">روح القدس<\/bdi>) here is Gabriel, peace be upon him: the holy spirit, an annexation of the qualified noun to its attribute. He is called this because he is pure of the turbidity of sense perception.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-61\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B057]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “We sent after” ( قفّينا ) means We caused to follow. ʿĪsā is a non-Arabic name adapted from Syriac Īshūʿ, declining without full inflection because it is a foreign proper name. Maryam means a servant, and its pattern is mafʿal , not faʿīl . “We strengthened him” means We fortified and supported him. The Holy Spirit ( روح القدس ) here is Gabriel, peace be upon him: the holy spirit, an annexation of the qualified noun to its attribute. He is called this because he is pure of the turbidity of sense perception. [B057]"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Rejection prompted by desire","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: We gave Moses the Torah, but you did not fulfil its right or act upon its contents. In care for you We sent messengers after him, another whenever one died. We gave Jesus son of Mary clear miracles: reviving the dead, healing congenital blindness and leprosy, reporting unseen things, and the Gospel. We supported him with Gabriel, who accompanied him wherever he went and raised him to heaven when you sought to kill him.<\/p><p>Whenever a messenger brought what your souls disliked—arduous obedience and abandonment of egoic shares and desires—did you grow proud and refuse belief, denying some, such as Jesus, Solomon and Muḥammad, peace be upon them, and killing others, such as Zechariah and John, peace be upon them? Al-Qushayrī says: “They listened to the callers with the ear of caprice, so their own attributes and desires became what they worshipped.” End of quotation.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: We gave Moses the Torah, but you did not fulfil its right or act upon its contents. In care for you We sent messengers after him, another whenever one died. We gave Jesus son of Mary clear miracles: reviving the dead, healing congenital blindness and leprosy, reporting unseen things, and the Gospel. We supported him with Gabriel, who accompanied him wherever he went and raised him to heaven when you sought to kill him. Whenever a messenger brought what your souls disliked—arduous obedience and abandonment of egoic shares and desires—did you grow proud and refuse belief, denying some, such as Jesus, Solomon and Muḥammad, peace be upon them, and killing others, such as Zechariah and John, peace be upon them? Al-Qushayrī says: “They listened to the callers with the ear of caprice, so their own attributes and desires became what they worshipped.” End of quotation."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The continuing callers to God","html":"<p>Everything the Real tells the Children of Israel in the manifest purport of the address He tells this community in its inward mystery. He gave us the Book, showing right guidance, and after its revelation sent reverent scholars and pure friends of God, judging by its judgement and guiding by its guidance. When they command renunciation of the world and abandonment of shares and desires, people reject and deny them, sometimes declaring them unbelievers and killing them, too proud to yield and accept their words. Some they deny; others they kill.<\/p><p>The hadith says: “You will follow the ways of those before you, span by span and cubit by cubit, so that if they entered a lizard’s hole, you would enter it.” They asked, “Who, Messenger of God—the Jews and Christians?” He replied, “Yes; who else?” That is: whom would you follow but them?<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-62\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B058]<\/a><\/p><p>Callers to God do not cease while the religion remains. Some call to His judgements, others to His knowledge: the first are scholars, the second His friends. When they command departure from habits and desires, people shoot at them the arrows of reproach and opposition. No one has brought the like of what they bring without being opposed—except that one singled out by antecedent care and visited by guidance’s breeze follows their traces. Few are such people.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Everything the Real tells the Children of Israel in the manifest purport of the address He tells this community in its inward mystery. He gave us the Book, showing right guidance, and after its revelation sent reverent scholars and pure friends of God, judging by its judgement and guiding by its guidance. When they command renunciation of the world and abandonment of shares and desires, people reject and deny them, sometimes declaring them unbelievers and killing them, too proud to yield and accept their words. Some they deny; others they kill. The hadith says: “You will follow the ways of those before you, span by span and cubit by cubit, so that if they entered a lizard’s hole, you would enter it.” They asked, “Who, Messenger of God—the Jews and Christians?” He replied, “Yes; who else?” That is: whom would you follow but them? [B058] Callers to God do not cease while the religion remains. Some call to His judgements, others to His knowledge: the first are scholars, the second His friends. When they command departure from habits and desires, people shoot at them the arrows of reproach and opposition. No one has brought the like of what they bring without being opposed—except that one singled out by antecedent care and visited by guidance’s breeze follows their traces. Few are such people."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High mentions their appalling assertion, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High mentions their appalling assertion, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2812,"arabic_characters":2244},{"id":"q2-88","q":"2:88","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:88 · Covered hearts and the refusal of discipline","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 131–132","cairo":"I:131–132","source_page_first":131,"source_page_last":132,"ar":"وَقالُوا قُلُوبُنا غُلْفٌ بَلْ لَعَنَهُمُ اللَّهُ بِكُفْرِهِمْ فَقَلِيلاً ما يُؤْمِنُونَ (88)\nقلت: (غلف) : جمع أغلف، كأحمر وحُمْر، وأصفر وصُفْر، وهو الذي عليه غشاوة، أي: هي في غلاف فلا تفقه ما تقول، بمنزلة الأغلف، وهو غير المختون، وقيل: أصله (غُلُف) بضم اللام، وبه قرأ ابن محيصن.\nفيكون جمع غلاف، كحجاب وحجب، وكتاب وكتب، ومعناه: قلوبنا أوعية لكل علم فلا نحتاج إلى علمك وكتابك.\nو (قليلاً) صفة لمحذوف أي: فإيماناً قليلاً، أو عدداً قليلاً يؤمنون، أو ظرف لأنه من صفة الأحيان، والعامل فيه ما يليه، و (ما) لتأكيد القلة، أي: في قليل من الأحيان يؤمنون، أو حال من الواو في (يؤمنون) أي: فيؤمنون في حال قلتهم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قالت اليهود استهزاء بما تدعوهم إليه: قُلُوبُنا مغلفة ومغشاة فلا نفقه ما تقول، أو أوعية للعلوم فلا تحتاج إلى علمك، قال الله تعالى: بَلْ لا غطاء على قلوبهم حساً، بل هي على الفطرة لكن لَعَنَهُمُ اللَّهُ وطردهم وخذلهم بسبب بِكُفْرِهِمْ فأبطل استعدادها للعلم، فَقَلِيلًا مَّا يُؤْمِنُونَ أي: فإيماناً قليلاً يؤمنون كإيمانهم ببعض الكتاب، أو فلا يؤمن إلا قليل منهم كعبد الله بن سلام وأصحابه، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إذا أمر الدعاة إلى الله أهل الدنيا بذبح النفوس وحط الرءوس ودفع الفلوس، ليتأهلوا به لدخول حضرة القدوس، أو أمروهم بخرق العوائد، لتخرق لهم العوائد ، أنفوا وعنفوا وقالوا: قلوبنا في أكنة مما تدعونا إليه، فيقال لهم: بل سبق لكم من الله البعد والحرمان، فأنكرتم أسباب الشهود والعيان، لكن مَن سبقت له من الله العناية، وهبَّ عليه نسيم الهداية، فلا تضره الجناية، فقد يلتحق بالخصوص، وإن كان من أعظم اللصوص، وهو قليل بالنسبة إلى من جاهد نفسه في طلب السبيل، وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُقْتَدِراً.\nثم وبخهم ولعنهم على عدم الإيمان بالقرآن مع إقرارهم به قبل الإتيان، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَقالُوا قُلُوبُنا غُلْفٌ بَلْ لَعَنَهُمُ اللَّهُ بِكُفْرِهِمْ فَقَلِيلاً ما يُؤْمِنُونَ (88)","quran_en":"88. They say, “Our hearts are covered.” Rather, God has cursed them for their unbelief; little indeed do they believe.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Covered hearts or repositories of knowledge","html":"<p>I say: <i>ghulf<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غُلْف<\/bdi>) is the plural of <i>aghlaf<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أغلف<\/bdi>), as <i>ḥumr<\/i> is of <i>aḥmar<\/i> and <i>ṣufr<\/i> of <i>aṣfar<\/i>. It means something upon which there is a covering: our hearts are in a sheath and do not understand what you say, like the <i>aghlaf<\/i>, the uncircumcised person. Alternatively, its original form is <i>ghuluf<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غُلُف<\/bdi>), with the lām vowelled <i>u<\/i>, as Ibn Muḥayṣin reads it.<\/p><p>It would then be the plural of <i>ghilāf<\/i>, “container,” like <i>ḥijāb/ḥujub<\/i> and <i>kitāb/kutub<\/i>: our hearts are repositories of every kind of knowledge, so we have no need of your knowledge or your Book.<\/p><p>“Little” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قليلاً<\/bdi>) qualifies an omitted expression: they believe with little faith, or a small number believe. Alternatively, it is adverbial, since it qualifies periods of time, governed by the following verb; <i>mā<\/i> emphasizes the paucity: on few occasions do they believe. Or it is a circumstantial expression relating to the plural subject of “believe”: they believe while few in number.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: ghulf ( غُلْف ) is the plural of aghlaf ( أغلف ), as ḥumr is of aḥmar and ṣufr of aṣfar . It means something upon which there is a covering: our hearts are in a sheath and do not understand what you say, like the aghlaf , the uncircumcised person. Alternatively, its original form is ghuluf ( غُلُف ), with the lām vowelled u , as Ibn Muḥayṣin reads it. It would then be the plural of ghilāf , “container,” like ḥijāb/ḥujub and kitāb/kutub : our hearts are repositories of every kind of knowledge, so we have no need of your knowledge or your Book. “Little” ( قليلاً ) qualifies an omitted expression: they believe with little faith, or a small number believe. Alternatively, it is adverbial, since it qualifies periods of time, governed by the following verb; mā emphasizes the paucity: on few occasions do they believe. Or it is a circumstantial expression relating to the plural subject of “believe”: they believe while few in number."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The original disposition and its frustration","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews said, mocking what you invite them to, “Our hearts are enclosed and covered, so we do not understand what you say”; or, “They contain the sciences and have no need of yours.” God Most High says: rather, there is no material covering over their hearts; they are constituted upon the original disposition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفطرة<\/bdi>). But God has cursed, expelled and forsaken them because of their unbelief, thus frustrating their receptivity to knowledge. They believe with little faith, as when they believe in part of the Book; or only a few believe, such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions. And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: the Jews said, mocking what you invite them to, “Our hearts are enclosed and covered, so we do not understand what you say”; or, “They contain the sciences and have no need of yours.” God Most High says: rather, there is no material covering over their hearts; they are constituted upon the original disposition ( الفطرة ). But God has cursed, expelled and forsaken them because of their unbelief, thus frustrating their receptivity to knowledge. They believe with little faith, as when they believe in part of the Book; or only a few believe, such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām and his companions. And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Breaking habits and the antecedent gift of guidance","html":"<p>When those who summon people to God tell the worldly to slaughter their lower selves, lower their heads and part with their money, that they may become fit to enter the presence of the All-Holy—or tell them to break their habitual patterns, so that ordinary patterns may be broken for them—they take offence, become harsh, and say, “Our hearts are enclosed against what you invite us to.”<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-63\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B059]<\/a> They are told: rather, distance and deprivation have preceded you from God, so you deny the means of witnessing and direct vision.<\/p><p>Yet one for whom divine care has preceded, and upon whom the breeze of guidance blows, is not ultimately barred by his wrongdoing: he may join the elect even if he has been among the greatest thieves. Such a case is rare in comparison with those who struggle against themselves in seeking the way. “God has power over everything” [18:45].<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When those who summon people to God tell the worldly to slaughter their lower selves, lower their heads and part with their money, that they may become fit to enter the presence of the All-Holy—or tell them to break their habitual patterns, so that ordinary patterns may be broken for them—they take offence, become harsh, and say, “Our hearts are enclosed against what you invite us to.” [B059] They are told: rather, distance and deprivation have preceded you from God, so you deny the means of witnessing and direct vision. Yet one for whom divine care has preceded, and upon whom the breeze of guidance blows, is not ultimately barred by his wrongdoing: he may join the elect even if he has been among the greatest thieves. Such a case is rare in comparison with those who struggle against themselves in seeking the way. “God has power over everything” [18:45]."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He rebukes and curses them for failing to believe in the Qurʾān, although they acknowledged it before its coming, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He rebukes and curses them for failing to believe in the Qurʾān, although they acknowledged it before its coming, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C132.1","page":132,"ar":"خرق العوائد الأولى هي خرق الحجب، من غفلة وظلمة قلب، وغير ذلك، قد يعطى بها الكرامات، وخرق العوائد الثانية هي خرق ما تعودته النفس وألفته حتى صعب خروجها عنه، ككثرة الأكل والشرب، وحب الجاه والرئاسة والمدح.","en":"The first “breaking of habitual patterns” is the breaking of veils—heedlessness, darkness of heart, and the like—through which miraculous gifts may be bestowed. The second “breaking of habitual patterns” is the breaking of what the lower self has grown accustomed and attached to, so that it finds departure from it difficult: much eating and drinking, love of prestige, leadership, and praise.","anchor":"بخرق العوائد لتخرق لهم العوائد"}],"english_characters":2593,"arabic_characters":1592},{"id":"q2-89","q":"2:89","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:89 · Recognizing what one subsequently rejects","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 132–133","cairo":"I:132–133","source_page_first":132,"source_page_last":133,"ar":"وَلَمَّا جاءَهُمْ كِتابٌ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ مُصَدِّقٌ لِما مَعَهُمْ وَكانُوا مِنْ قَبْلُ يَسْتَفْتِحُونَ عَلَى الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا فَلَمَّا جاءَهُمْ ما عَرَفُوا كَفَرُوا بِهِ فَلَعْنَةُ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكافِرِينَ (89)\nقلت: (لمَّا) حرف وجود لوجود إذا وليها الماضي، ولها شرط وجواب، وهو هنا محذوف دلّ عليه جواب (لما) الثانية، أي: ولما جاءهم كتاب من عند الله كفروا به، أو (لما) الثانية تأكيد للأولى. والجواب: (كفروا به) ، أو فلما وجوابها جواب الأولى، كقوله فَإِمَّا يَأْتِيَنَّكُمْ مِنِّي هُدىً فَمَنِ اتَّبَعَ ... الآية، و (يستفتحون) ينتصرون، وفي الحديث: «أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلّم كان يَستَفْتِحُ بِصعَالِيكِ المُهَاجِرينَ» ، الذين لا مال لهم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَمَّا جاءَهُمْ أي: اليهود، القرآن مصدقاً لِما مَعَهُمْ من التوراة، أي:\nموافقاً له وشاهداً له بالصحة، وقد كانوا قبل ظهوره يستنصرون على أعدائهم بالنبيّ الذي جاء به، فيقولون: اللهم انصرنا عليهم بالنبيّ المبعوث في آخر الزمان، الذي نجد نعته في التوراة، وكانوا يقولون لأعدائهم من المشركين:\n(قد أظلَّ زمانُ نبيّ يخرج بتصديق ما قلنا، فنقتلكم معه قتل عاد وإرَم) ، فلما ظهر وعرفوه كفروا به فَلَعْنَةُ اللَّهِ عليهم، فوضع الظاهر موضع المضمر للدلالة على أنهم لُعنوا لكفرهم، فاللام في الْكافِرِينَ للعهد، وهم كفار اليهود، أو للجنس، فتكون اللعنة عامة لكل كافر، ويدخلون فيها دخولا أولياء، والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: ترى كثيراً من الناس إذا ذكر لهم الأولياء المتقدمون أقروهم وصدقوهم، وإذا ذكر لهم أولياء أهل زمانهم أنكروهم وجحدوهم، مع كونهم يستنصرون بأهل زمانهم في الجملة. فهذه نزعة يهودية، آمنوا ببعض وكفروا ببعض.\nوالناس في إثبات الخصوصية ونفيها على ثلاثة أقسام: قسم أثبتوها للمتقدمين، ونفوها عن المتأخرين، وهم أقبح العوام، وقسم أقروها قديماً وحديثاً، وقالوا: إنهم أخفياء في زمانهم، فحرمهم الله بركتهم، وقوم أقروا الخصوصية في أهل زمانهم، وعرفوهم وظفروا بهم وعظموهم، وهم السعداء الذين أراد الله أن يوصلهم إليه ويقربهم إلى حضرته.\nوفي الحكم: «سبحان مَن لم يجعل الدليل على أوليائه إلا من حيث الدليل عليه، ولم يوصل إليهم إلا مَن أراد أن يوصله إليه» . وبالله التوفيق.\nثم أشار الحق تعالى إلى تسفيه رأى اليهود حيث استبدلوا الإيمان بالكفر، والربح بالخسران، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَمَّا جاءَهُمْ كِتابٌ مِنْ عِنْدِ اللَّهِ مُصَدِّقٌ لِما مَعَهُمْ وَكانُوا مِنْ قَبْلُ يَسْتَفْتِحُونَ عَلَى الَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا فَلَمَّا جاءَهُمْ ما عَرَفُوا كَفَرُوا بِهِ فَلَعْنَةُ اللَّهِ عَلَى الْكافِرِينَ (89)","quran_en":"89. When a Book came to them from God, confirming what they possessed—although before this they had sought victory over the unbelievers—when what they recognized came to them, they disbelieved in it. God’s curse is upon the unbelievers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Lammā and seeking victory","html":"<p>I say: when followed by a past verb, <i>lammā<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لمّا<\/bdi>) is a particle indicating the occurrence of one thing upon the occurrence of another. It has a conditional clause and a response. Here the response is omitted, indicated by that of the second <i>lammā<\/i>: when a Book came to them from God, they disbelieved in it. Alternatively, the second <i>lammā<\/i> confirms the first, and “they disbelieved in it” is the response; or the second <i>lammā<\/i> with its response answers the first, as in “When guidance comes to you from Me, then whoever follows…” [2:38]. “They sought an opening” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يستفتحون<\/bdi>) means they sought victory. In the hadith: “The Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, used to seek victory through the impoverished Emigrants”—those without possessions.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: when followed by a past verb, lammā ( لمّا ) is a particle indicating the occurrence of one thing upon the occurrence of another. It has a conditional clause and a response. Here the response is omitted, indicated by that of the second lammā : when a Book came to them from God, they disbelieved in it. Alternatively, the second lammā confirms the first, and “they disbelieved in it” is the response; or the second lammā with its response answers the first, as in “When guidance comes to you from Me, then whoever follows…” [2:38]. “They sought an opening” ( يستفتحون ) means they sought victory. In the hadith: “The Prophet, God bless him and grant him peace, used to seek victory through the impoverished Emigrants”—those without possessions."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Prior expectation and subsequent unbelief","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when the Qurʾān came to the Jews, confirming the Torah they possessed—that is, agreeing with it and bearing witness to its truth—they had previously sought victory over their enemies through the Prophet who brought it. They would say, “O God, give us victory over them through the Prophet sent at the end of time, whose description we find in the Torah.” To their polytheist enemies they used to say, “The time of a prophet is now near: he will emerge confirming what we have said, and with him we shall slay you as ʿĀd and Iram were slain.” But when he appeared and they recognized him, they disbelieved in him. God’s curse is therefore upon them.<\/p><p>An explicit noun replaces the expected pronoun to indicate that they were cursed for unbelief. The article in “the unbelievers” is either referential, indicating the unbelieving Jews in question, or generic: the curse then encompasses every unbeliever, and they are included first and foremost.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-64\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B060]<\/a> And God Most High knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: when the Qurʾān came to the Jews, confirming the Torah they possessed—that is, agreeing with it and bearing witness to its truth—they had previously sought victory over their enemies through the Prophet who brought it. They would say, “O God, give us victory over them through the Prophet sent at the end of time, whose description we find in the Torah.” To their polytheist enemies they used to say, “The time of a prophet is now near: he will emerge confirming what we have said, and with him we shall slay you as ʿĀd and Iram were slain.” But when he appeared and they recognized him, they disbelieved in him. God’s curse is therefore upon them. An explicit noun replaces the expected pronoun to indicate that they were cursed for unbelief. The article in “the unbelievers” is either referential, indicating the unbelieving Jews in question, or generic: the curse then encompasses every unbeliever, and they are included first and foremost. [B060] And God Most High knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Recognizing the friends of God in one’s own time","html":"<p>You see many people acknowledge and affirm the friends of God of earlier times when they are mentioned, yet deny and reject the friends of God of their own time—even though in a general way they seek victory through contemporaries. This is a tendency resembling the Jews’ conduct: believing in some and disbelieving in others.<\/p><p>People fall into three classes concerning the affirmation and denial of special election (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الخصوصية<\/bdi>). One class affirms it for the earlier generations and denies it to the later; these are the worst of the common people. Another acknowledges it in both earlier and later times, but says that its possessors are hidden in their own time: God deprives them of their blessing. A third acknowledges special election in people of its own time, recognizes, finds and reveres them. These are the fortunate whom God wishes to bring to Himself and draw near to His presence.<\/p><p>In the <i>Ḥikam<\/i>: “Glory to Him who has made the indication of His friends arise only from the indication of Himself, and who brings to them only those whom He wishes to bring to Himself.” Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"You see many people acknowledge and affirm the friends of God of earlier times when they are mentioned, yet deny and reject the friends of God of their own time—even though in a general way they seek victory through contemporaries. This is a tendency resembling the Jews’ conduct: believing in some and disbelieving in others. People fall into three classes concerning the affirmation and denial of special election ( الخصوصية ). One class affirms it for the earlier generations and denies it to the later; these are the worst of the common people. Another acknowledges it in both earlier and later times, but says that its possessors are hidden in their own time: God deprives them of their blessing. A third acknowledges special election in people of its own time, recognizes, finds and reveres them. These are the fortunate whom God wishes to bring to Himself and draw near to His presence. In the Ḥikam : “Glory to Him who has made the indication of His friends arise only from the indication of Himself, and who brings to them only those whom He wishes to bring to Himself.” Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High points to the foolishness of the Jews’ judgement in exchanging faith for unbelief and profit for loss, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High points to the foolishness of the Jews’ judgement in exchanging faith for unbelief and profit for loss, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3004,"arabic_characters":2007},{"id":"q2-90-91","q":"2:90–91","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:90–91 · Envy of divine favour and the relation of Law to Reality","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 133–135","cairo":"I:133–135","source_page_first":133,"source_page_last":135,"ar":"بِئْسَمَا اشْتَرَوْا بِهِ أَنْفُسَهُمْ أَنْ يَكْفُرُوا بِما أَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ بَغْياً أَنْ يُنَزِّلَ اللَّهُ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ عَلى مَنْ يَشاءُ مِنْ عِبادِهِ فَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ عَلى غَضَبٍ وَلِلْكافِرِينَ عَذابٌ مُهِينٌ (90) وَإِذا قِيلَ لَهُمْ آمِنُوا بِما أَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ قالُوا نُؤْمِنُ بِما أُنْزِلَ عَلَيْنا وَيَكْفُرُونَ بِما وَراءَهُ وَهُوَ الْحَقُّ مُصَدِّقاً لِما مَعَهُمْ قُلْ فَلِمَ تَقْتُلُونَ أَنْبِياءَ اللَّهِ مِنْ قَبْلُ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (91)\nقلت: بئس ونعم: فعلان جامدان مختصان بالدخول على ما يدل على العموم، إما نكرة، فتنصب على التمييز المفسر للضمير الفاعل، أو معرف بأل الجنسية، فيرتفع على الفاعلية، تقول: بئس رجلاً زيدٌ، وبئس الرجل زيد، ويذكر بعد ذلك المخصوص: إما خبر عن مبتدأ مضمر، أو مبتدأ والخبر مقدم. وإنما اخْتُصَّتَا بالدخول على ما يدل على العموم لأن (نعم) مستوفية لجميع المدح، و (بئس) مستوفية لجميع الذم. فإذا قلت: نعم الرجل زيد، فكأنك قلت: استحق زيدٌ المدحَ الذي يكون في سائر جنسه، وكذلك تقول في بئس.\nو (ما) المتصلة ببئس ونعم: نكرة منصوبة على التمييز، أي: بئس شيئاً اشتروا به أنفسهم، وهو كفرهم، أو معرفة تامة مرفوعة على الفاعل، أي: بئس الشيء شيء اشتروا به أنفسهم. و (اشتروا) هنا بمعنى باعوا، كشَروا على خلاف الأصل، وقد يمكن ان يبقى على أصله، على ما يأتي في بيان المعنى.\nو (بغيا) مفعول من اجله ليكفروا، و (يكفرون) حال من الفاعل في (قالوا) ، و (وراء) في الأصل: مصدر جُعل ظرفاً، ويضاف إلى الفاعل ويراد به ما يتوارى به وهو خلفه، وإلى المفعول فيراد به ما يواريه وهو قدامه، ولذلك عد من الأضداد، قاله البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله في شأن اليهود: بئس شيئاً باعوا به حظ أنفسهم، وهو كفرهم بما أنزل الله، أو بِئْسَمَا اشْتَرَوْا بِهِ أَنْفُسَهُمْ بحسب ظنهم، فإنهم ظنوا أنهم خلّصوا أنفسهم من العذاب بما فعلوا، وهو كفرهم بما أنزل الله على محمد نبيه صلى الله عليه وسلّم بغياً وحسداً أن يكون النبيّ من غيرهم، فانقلبوا بِغَضَبٍ عَلى غَضَبٍ للكفر والحسد لمن هو أفضل الخلق، أو لكفرهم بمحمد- عليه الصلاة والسلام- بعد عيسى عليه السلام، أو لتضييعهم التوراة، وكفرهم بمحمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم، وَلِلْكافِرِينَ عَذابٌ مُهِينٌ أي: يذلهم ويخزيهم في الدنيا والآخرة، بخلاف عذاب العاصي فإنه كفارة لذنوبه.\nوَإِذا قِيلَ لهؤلاء اليهود: آمِنُوا بِما أَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ على محمد صلى الله عليه وسلّم قالُوا نُؤْمِنُ بِما أُنْزِلَ عَلَيْنا من التوراة، وهم يَكْفُرُونَ بِما وَراءَهُ أي: بما سواه، وهو القرآن، حال كونه مُصَدِّقاً لِما مَعَهُمْ من التوراة ومهيمناً عليه. قُلْ لهم يا محمد: فَلِمَ تَقْتُلُونَ أَنْبِياءَ اللَّهِ مِنْ قَبْلُ هذا الزمان، وهو محرم عليكم في التوراة، إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ به؟ فهذا يبطل دعواكم الإيمان بالتوراة إذ الإيمان بالكتاب يقتضي العمل به، وإلاَّ كان دعوى، وإن فعله أسلافكم فأنتم راضون به وعازمون عليه.\nالإشارة: اعلم أن قاعدة تفسير أهل الإشارة هي أن كل عتاب توجه لمن ترك طريق الإيمان، وأنكر على أهله يتوجه مثله لمن ترك طريق مقام الإحسان، وأنكر على أهله. وكل وعيد توعد به أهل الكفران يتوعد به مَن ترك السلوك لمقام الإحسان، غير أن عذاب أهل الكفر حسي بدني، وعذاب أهل الحجاب معنوي قلبي.\nفنقول فيمن رضي بعيبه وأقام على مرض قلبه وأنكر الأطباء ووجود أهل التربية: بئسما اشتروا به أنفسهم، وهو كفرهم بما أنزل الله من الخصوصية على قلوب أوليائه بغياً وحسداً، أو جهلاً وسوء ظن، أن ينزل الله من فضله على\nمن يشاء من عباده، فباءوا بغضب الحجاب على غضب البعد والارتياب، أو بغضب سقم القلوب على غضب الإصرار على المساوئ والعيوب. (من لم يتغلغل في علمنا هذا مات مُصرًا على الكبائر وهو لا يشعر) كما قال الشاذلي رضي الله عنه، ولا يصح التغلغل فيه إلا بصحبة أهله. وللكافرين بالخصوصية عذاب الطمع وسجن الأكوان، وهما شجرة الذل والهوان.\nوإذا قيل لهم: آمنوا بما أنزل الله من أسرار الحقيقة وأنوار الطريقة، قالوا: نؤمن بما أنزل علينا من ظواهر الشريعة، ويكفرون بما وراءه من أسرار الحقيقة، ككشف أسرار الذات وأنوار الصفات، وهو- أي: علم الحقيقة- الحق لأنه خالص لب الشريعة، ولله در صاحب المباحث الأصلية حيث قال:\nهل ظاهِرُ الشرع وعلمُ الباطِنْ ... إلا كجِسمٍ فيه رُوحٌ سَاكِنْ؟\nوقال أيضاً:\nما مَثلُ المعقولِ والمنقولِ ... إلا كَدُرِّ زاخرٍ مَجْهُولِ\nحتى إذا أخْرَجَهُ الغِوَّاصُ ... لم يكُ لِلدُرِّ إذن خلاص\nوإنما خَلاصُهُ في الكَشْفِ ... عن الغِطَاءِ حيثُ لا يسْتَخْفِي\nفَالصّدَفُ الظاهرُ ثم الدرُّ ... مَعْقولُه والجهلُ ذاك البحْرُ\nوكان الشيخ عز الدين بن عبد السلام يقول: (هل ثَمَّ شيءٌ غيرُ ما فهمناه من الكتاب والسنة؟) ، كان يقول ذلك إذا قيل له: إن الشيخ الشاذلي فاض اليوم بعلوم وأسرار، فلما التقى بالشيخ وأخذ بيده، قال: (أي والله.. ما قعد على قواعد الشريعة التي لا تنهدم إلا الصوفية) . ويقال لمن ادعى التمسك بالشريعة وأنكر ما وراءها: فلم تشتغل بجمع الدنيا واحتكارها وتخاف من الفقر، وتهتم بأمر الرزق وتجزع من المصائب، والشريعة تنادي عليك بذم ذلك كله إن كنت مؤمناً؟!! وبالله التوفيق.\nثم نعى عليهم عبادة العجل بعد ما رأوا من الآيات البينات، إبطالا لدعواهم الإيمان بالتوراة، فقال:","quran_ar":"بِئْسَمَا اشْتَرَوْا بِهِ أَنْفُسَهُمْ أَنْ يَكْفُرُوا بِما أَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ بَغْياً أَنْ يُنَزِّلَ اللَّهُ مِنْ فَضْلِهِ عَلى مَنْ يَشاءُ مِنْ عِبادِهِ فَباؤُ بِغَضَبٍ عَلى غَضَبٍ وَلِلْكافِرِينَ عَذابٌ مُهِينٌ (90) وَإِذا قِيلَ لَهُمْ آمِنُوا بِما أَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ قالُوا نُؤْمِنُ بِما أُنْزِلَ عَلَيْنا وَيَكْفُرُونَ بِما وَراءَهُ وَهُوَ الْحَقُّ مُصَدِّقاً لِما مَعَهُمْ قُلْ فَلِمَ تَقْتُلُونَ أَنْبِياءَ اللَّهِ مِنْ قَبْلُ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (91)","quran_en":"90. Wretched is that for which they have sold their souls: disbelieving in what God has sent down, out of envy that God should bestow His bounty upon whomsoever He wills among His servants. They have incurred wrath upon wrath; for the unbelievers is a humiliating punishment. 91. When they are told, “Believe in what God has sent down,” they say, “We believe in what was sent down to us,” while disbelieving in what came after it, although it is the truth, confirming what they possess. Say, “Why then did you kill God’s prophets before, if you were believers?”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The comprehensive force of praise and blame","html":"<p>I say: <i>biʾsa<\/i> and <i>niʿma<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بئس، نعم<\/bdi>) are invariable verbs restricted to expressions indicating generality. This may be an indefinite noun, accusative as a specification (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تمييز<\/bdi>) explaining the pronominal agent, or a noun with the generic article, nominative as the agent (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فاعل<\/bdi>). Thus: <i>biʾsa rajulan Zaydun<\/i>, “What a bad man Zayd is”; or <i>biʾsa al-rajulu Zaydun<\/i>. The particular person praised or blamed follows, either as the predicate of an implied subject or as a subject with its predicate preceding. These verbs are restricted to general expressions because <i>niʿma<\/i> exhausts every aspect of praise, and <i>biʾsa<\/i> every aspect of blame. “What an excellent man Zayd is” is as though you said: Zayd deserves the praise belonging to his whole kind. The corresponding explanation applies to <i>biʾsa<\/i>.<\/p><p>The <i>mā<\/i> attached to <i>biʾsa<\/i> or <i>niʿma<\/i> is an indefinite expression, accusative as specification: “What a wretched thing it is for which they sold their souls”—their unbelief. Or it is a complete definite expression, nominative as the agent: “Wretched is the thing—the thing for which they sold their souls.” Here <i>ishtaraw<\/i> means “sold,” as <i>sharaw<\/i> does, contrary to its usual meaning. It may, however, retain its usual meaning, “bought,” as will appear in the exposition.<\/p><p>“Out of envy” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بغياً<\/bdi>) is an accusative of purpose explaining their unbelief. “They disbelieve” is circumstantial, relating to the subject of “they said.” <i>Warāʾ<\/i> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وراء<\/bdi>) was originally a verbal noun made into an adverb. When annexed to the agent, it denotes that by which he is hidden, lying behind him; when annexed to the object, it denotes what hides it, lying before it. Hence it is counted among expressions bearing opposite meanings. So al-Bayḍāwī explains.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: biʾsa and niʿma ( بئس، نعم ) are invariable verbs restricted to expressions indicating generality. This may be an indefinite noun, accusative as a specification ( تمييز ) explaining the pronominal agent, or a noun with the generic article, nominative as the agent ( فاعل ). Thus: biʾsa rajulan Zaydun , “What a bad man Zayd is”; or biʾsa al-rajulu Zaydun . The particular person praised or blamed follows, either as the predicate of an implied subject or as a subject with its predicate preceding. These verbs are restricted to general expressions because niʿma exhausts every aspect of praise, and biʾsa every aspect of blame. “What an excellent man Zayd is” is as though you said: Zayd deserves the praise belonging to his whole kind. The corresponding explanation applies to biʾsa . The mā attached to biʾsa or niʿma is an indefinite expression, accusative as specification: “What a wretched thing it is for which they sold their souls”—their unbelief. Or it is a complete definite expression, nominative as the agent: “Wretched is the thing—the thing for which they sold their souls.” Here ishtaraw means “sold,” as sharaw does, contrary to its usual meaning. It may, however, retain its usual meaning, “bought,” as will appear in the exposition. “Out of envy” ( بغياً ) is an accusative of purpose explaining their unbelief. “They disbelieve” is circumstantial, relating to the subject of “they said.” Warāʾ ( وراء ) was originally a verbal noun made into an adverb. When annexed to the agent, it denotes that by which he is hidden, lying behind him; when annexed to the object, it denotes what hides it, lying before it. Hence it is counted among expressions bearing opposite meanings. So al-Bayḍāwī explains."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Faith professed but contradicted in practice","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says concerning the Jews: wretched is the thing for which they sold their souls’ portion—unbelief in what God sent down. Alternatively, wretched is that by which, as they imagined, they purchased their souls’ deliverance: they supposed that their action saved them from punishment. Their action was to disbelieve in what God revealed to His Prophet Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, through transgression and envy that the Prophet should come from a people other than themselves.<\/p><p>They returned with wrath upon wrath: for unbelief and envy towards the best of creation; or for disbelieving in Muḥammad after Jesus, peace be upon them; or for neglecting the Torah and disbelieving in Muḥammad. The unbelievers have a humiliating punishment, abasing and disgracing them in this world and the next, unlike the punishment of a disobedient believer, which expiates his sins.<\/p><p>When these Jews are told to believe what God revealed to Muḥammad, they say that they believe the Torah revealed to them, while disbelieving what lies beyond it—the Qurʾān—which confirms and stands as guardian over the Torah they possess. Say to them, Muḥammad: why did you kill God’s prophets before this time, when the Torah forbids it, if you believe in that Book? This invalidates your claim: believing a Book entails acting upon it; otherwise it is merely a claim. Although your forefathers committed the deed, you approve it and resolve upon the same course.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says concerning the Jews: wretched is the thing for which they sold their souls’ portion—unbelief in what God sent down. Alternatively, wretched is that by which, as they imagined, they purchased their souls’ deliverance: they supposed that their action saved them from punishment. Their action was to disbelieve in what God revealed to His Prophet Muḥammad, God bless him and grant him peace, through transgression and envy that the Prophet should come from a people other than themselves. They returned with wrath upon wrath: for unbelief and envy towards the best of creation; or for disbelieving in Muḥammad after Jesus, peace be upon them; or for neglecting the Torah and disbelieving in Muḥammad. The unbelievers have a humiliating punishment, abasing and disgracing them in this world and the next, unlike the punishment of a disobedient believer, which expiates his sins. When these Jews are told to believe what God revealed to Muḥammad, they say that they believe the Torah revealed to them, while disbelieving what lies beyond it—the Qurʾān—which confirms and stands as guardian over the Torah they possess. Say to them, Muḥammad: why did you kill God’s prophets before this time, when the Torah forbids it, if you believe in that Book? This invalidates your claim: believing a Book entails acting upon it; otherwise it is merely a claim. Although your forefathers committed the deed, you approve it and resolve upon the same course."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The rule of allusive interpretation","html":"<p>Know that the rule of those who interpret by allusion is this: every reproach addressed to those who abandon the way of faith and deny its people is addressed correspondingly to those who abandon the way of the station of spiritual excellence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الإحسان<\/bdi>) and deny its people. Every threat directed at unbelievers is correspondingly directed at those who abandon the journey to the station of spiritual excellence. But the unbelievers’ punishment is sensible and bodily, whereas the punishment of the veiled is inward and belongs to the heart.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-65\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B061]<\/a><\/p><p>Thus we say of those content with their faults, persisting in the sickness of their hearts, denying the physicians and the existence of masters of formation: wretched is that for which they have sold their souls—denial of the special election God bestows upon His friends’ hearts, through envy and transgression, or ignorance and bad opinion, that God should bestow His bounty<\/p><p>upon whomsoever He wills. They incur the wrath of veiling upon that of distance and uncertainty, or the wrath of sick hearts upon that of persistence in faults and defects. As al-Shādhilī, God be pleased with him, said: “Whoever does not penetrate this science of ours dies persisting in grave sins without knowing it.” One cannot properly penetrate it without accompanying its people. Those who deny special election suffer the punishment of covetousness and the prison of created things: these are the tree of abasement and humiliation.<\/p><p>When told to believe in the secrets of Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/bdi>) and lights of the Path (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الطريقة<\/bdi>) that God has bestowed, they say, “We believe in the outward ordinances of the Sacred Law revealed to us,” while denying what lies beyond them: the secrets of Reality, such as the unveiling of the Essence’s mysteries and the lights of the Attributes. Yet the science of Reality is the truth, because it is the pure kernel of the Sacred Law. How fine are the words of the author of <i>al-Mabāḥith al-aṣliyya<\/i>:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Are the outward Law and inward science<br>anything but a body in which a spirit dwells?<\/blockquote><p>He also says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">The intelligible and the transmitted are like<br>a pearl in an unknown, overflowing sea.<br>Even when the diver brings it forth,<br>the pearl is not yet free.<br>It is freed only by uncovering it,<br>removing the covering, so it lies concealed no more.<br>The shell is the outward, the pearl its intelligible meaning;<br>ignorance is that sea.<\/blockquote><p>Shaykh ʿIzz al-Dīn ibn ʿAbd al-Salām used to say, “Is there anything besides what we have understood from the Book and the Sunna?” He said this when told that Shaykh al-Shādhilī had that day poured forth sciences and mysteries. But when he met the shaykh and took his hand, he said: “Yes, by God! None but the Sufis are established upon the indestructible foundations of the Sacred Law.”<\/p><p>To someone claiming adherence to the Sacred Law while denying what lies beyond it, one says: why occupy yourself with amassing and hoarding the world, fear poverty, worry about provision and panic at calamities, when the Sacred Law proclaims its condemnation of all this—if you are a believer? Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Know that the rule of those who interpret by allusion is this: every reproach addressed to those who abandon the way of faith and deny its people is addressed correspondingly to those who abandon the way of the station of spiritual excellence ( الإحسان ) and deny its people. Every threat directed at unbelievers is correspondingly directed at those who abandon the journey to the station of spiritual excellence. But the unbelievers’ punishment is sensible and bodily, whereas the punishment of the veiled is inward and belongs to the heart. [B061] Thus we say of those content with their faults, persisting in the sickness of their hearts, denying the physicians and the existence of masters of formation: wretched is that for which they have sold their souls—denial of the special election God bestows upon His friends’ hearts, through envy and transgression, or ignorance and bad opinion, that God should bestow His bounty upon whomsoever He wills. They incur the wrath of veiling upon that of distance and uncertainty, or the wrath of sick hearts upon that of persistence in faults and defects. As al-Shādhilī, God be pleased with him, said: “Whoever does not penetrate this science of ours dies persisting in grave sins without knowing it.” One cannot properly penetrate it without accompanying its people. Those who deny special election suffer the punishment of covetousness and the prison of created things: these are the tree of abasement and humiliation. When told to believe in the secrets of Reality ( الحقيقة ) and lights of the Path ( الطريقة ) that God has bestowed, they say, “We believe in the outward ordinances of the Sacred Law revealed to us,” while denying what lies beyond them: the secrets of Reality, such as the unveiling of the Essence’s mysteries and the lights of the Attributes. Yet the science of Reality is the truth, because it is the pure kernel of the Sacred Law. How fine are the words of the author of al-Mabāḥith al-aṣliyya : Are the outward Law and inward science anything but a body in which a spirit dwells? He also says: The intelligible and the transmitted are like a pearl in an unknown, overflowing sea. Even when the diver brings it forth, the pearl is not yet free. It is freed only by uncovering it, removing the covering, so it lies concealed no more. The shell is the outward, the pearl its intelligible meaning; ignorance is that sea. Shaykh ʿIzz al-Dīn ibn ʿAbd al-Salām used to say, “Is there anything besides what we have understood from the Book and the Sunna?” He said this when told that Shaykh al-Shādhilī had that day poured forth sciences and mysteries. But when he met the shaykh and took his hand, he said: “Yes, by God! None but the Sufis are established upon the indestructible foundations of the Sacred Law.” To someone claiming adherence to the Sacred Law while denying what lies beyond it, one says: why occupy yourself with amassing and hoarding the world, fear poverty, worry about provision and panic at calamities, when the Sacred Law proclaims its condemnation of all this—if you are a believer? Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He reproaches them for worshipping the calf after seeing the clear signs, invalidating their claim to believe the Torah, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He reproaches them for worshipping the calf after seeing the clear signs, invalidating their claim to believe the Torah, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":6421,"arabic_characters":4465},{"id":"q2-92","q":"2:92","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:92 · Clear signs and the idol of desire","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 135–136","cairo":"I:135–136","source_page_first":135,"source_page_last":136,"ar":"وَلَقَدْ جاءَكُمْ مُوسى بِالْبَيِّناتِ ثُمَّ اتَّخَذْتُمُ الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ (92)\nقلت: جملة: وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ حال من (اتخذتم) .\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَقَدْ جاءَكُمْ مُوسى بالمعجزات الواضحات: كالعصا واليد وفلق البحر، ثم لم ينجح ذلك فيكم، فاتخذتم العجل إلهاً تعبدونه من بعد ذهابه إلى الطور وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ في ذلك، فأين دعواكم الإيمان بالتوراة؟\nالإشارة: ويقال لمن أقام على عيبه، ورضي بمرض قلبه، حتى لقي الله بقلب سقيم: لقد جاءتكم أوليائي بالآيات الواضحات، ولو لم يكن إلا شفاء المرضى على أيديهم- أعني مرضى القلوب- لكان كافياً، ثم اتخذتم الهوى إلهكم، وعبدتم العاجلة بقلوبكم، وعزَّتْ عليكم نفوسكم وفلوسكم، وأنتم ظالمون في الإقامة على مساوئكم وعيوبكم، مع وجود الطبيب لمن طلب الشفاء، وحسّن الظن وشهد الصفاء. (كن طالباً تجد مرشداً) وبالله التوفيق.\nثم عدّد الحق تعالى عليهم مساوئ تقدمت لأسلافهم تبطل دعوى إيمانهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَقَدْ جاءَكُمْ مُوسى بِالْبَيِّناتِ ثُمَّ اتَّخَذْتُمُ الْعِجْلَ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ وَأَنْتُمْ ظالِمُونَ (92)","quran_en":"92. Moses certainly came to you with clear signs; then, in his absence, you took the calf for worship, doing wrong.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The circumstantial clause","html":"<p>I say: “while you were wrongdoers” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وأنتم ظالمون<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial clause relating to “you took.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “while you were wrongdoers” ( وأنتم ظالمون ) is a circumstantial clause relating to “you took.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Signs that did not bear fruit","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Moses came to you with manifest miracles, such as the staff, the hand and the parting of the sea. Yet they did not produce their effect in you: after he went to the Mount you took the calf as a god to worship, doing wrong. Where, then, is your claim to believe the Torah?<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: Moses came to you with manifest miracles, such as the staff, the hand and the parting of the sea. Yet they did not produce their effect in you: after he went to the Mount you took the calf as a god to worship, doing wrong. Where, then, is your claim to believe the Torah?"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The available physician and the refusal of healing","html":"<p>One also says to a person who persists in his fault and accepts the sickness of his heart until he meets God with a sick heart: My friends came to you with clear signs. Even if they did no more than heal the sick—I mean those whose hearts are sick—that would suffice. Yet you made desire your god and worshipped the immediate world with your hearts. Your selves and your money were too dear to you, and you wronged yourselves by persisting in your defects when the physician was present for whoever sought healing, thought well and witnessed purity. “Be a seeker and you will find a guide.” Through God is success.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"One also says to a person who persists in his fault and accepts the sickness of his heart until he meets God with a sick heart: My friends came to you with clear signs. Even if they did no more than heal the sick—I mean those whose hearts are sick—that would suffice. Yet you made desire your god and worshipped the immediate world with your hearts. Your selves and your money were too dear to you, and you wronged yourselves by persisting in your defects when the physician was present for whoever sought healing, thought well and witnessed purity. “Be a seeker and you will find a guide.” Through God is success."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>The Real Most High recounts earlier faults of their forefathers that invalidate their claim to faith, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real Most High recounts earlier faults of their forefathers that invalidate their claim to faith, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1137,"arabic_characters":846},{"id":"q2-93","q":"2:93","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:93 · The heart imbued with love of the calf","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 136–137","cairo":"I:136–137","source_page_first":136,"source_page_last":137,"ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَكُمُ الطُّورَ خُذُوا ما آتَيْناكُمْ بِقُوَّةٍ وَاسْمَعُوا قالُوا سَمِعْنا وَعَصَيْنا وَأُشْرِبُوا فِي قُلُوبِهِمُ الْعِجْلَ بِكُفْرِهِمْ قُلْ بِئْسَما يَأْمُرُكُمْ بِهِ إِيمانُكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (93)\nقلت: (إن كنتم) : شرط حُذف جوابه، أي: إن كنتم مؤمنين فبئس ما يأمركم به إيمانكم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَاذكروا أيضاً إِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ أن تعملوا بالتوراة فأبيتم وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَكُمُ جبل الطُّورَ وقلنا: خُذُوا ما آتَيْناكُمْ بِقُوَّةٍ واجتهاد وَاسْمَعُوا ما أقول لكم فيه قالُوا بلسان حالهم: سَمِعْنا قولك وعَصَيْنا أمرك، حيث لم يمتثلوا، أو بلسان المقال لسوء أدبهم، وَأُشْرِبُوا فِي قُلُوبِهِمُ حب الْعِجْلَ حتى صبغ فيها ورسخ رسوخ الصبغ في الثوب، لأنهم كانوا مُجَسِّمَةً، ولم يروا منظراً أعجب من العجل الذي صنعه السامري، (قل) لهم يا محمد: بِئْسَما يَأْمُرُكُمْ بِهِ إِيمانُكُمْ بالتوراة الذي ادعيتموه، إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ، لكن الإيمان لا يأمر بهذا فلستم مؤمنين.\nالإشارة: يقول الحق جلّ جلاله لمن ادعى كمال الإيمان، وهو منكر على أهل الإحسان، مع إقامته على عوائد نفسه، وكونه محجوباً بشهود حسه: وإذ أخذنا ميثاقكم، بأن تجاهدوا نفوسكم، وتخرقوا عوائدكم لتدخلوا حضرة\nربكم، ورفعنا فوق رؤوسكم سيوف التخويف، أو جبال التشويق، وأوضحنا لكم سواء الطريق، وقلنا لكم: خذوا ما آتيناكم من خرق العوائد، واكتساب الفوائد، بجد واجتهاد، فأبيتم وعزَّت عليكم نُفوسكم، وقلتم بلسان حالكم:\nسمعنا وعصينا، وأشربت قلوبكم حب العاجلة، وآثرتم الدنيا على الآخرة، بئسما يأمركم به إيمانكم إن كنتم مؤمنين.\nومن جملة ما ادعاه اليهود اختصاصهم بالجنة، فردَّ الله عليهم بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَإِذْ أَخَذْنا مِيثاقَكُمْ وَرَفَعْنا فَوْقَكُمُ الطُّورَ خُذُوا ما آتَيْناكُمْ بِقُوَّةٍ وَاسْمَعُوا قالُوا سَمِعْنا وَعَصَيْنا وَأُشْرِبُوا فِي قُلُوبِهِمُ الْعِجْلَ بِكُفْرِهِمْ قُلْ بِئْسَما يَأْمُرُكُمْ بِهِ إِيمانُكُمْ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ (93)","quran_en":"93. When We took your covenant and raised the Mount above you: “Hold firmly to what We have given you, and listen.” They said, “We hear and disobey.” Through their unbelief their hearts were imbued with the calf. Say, “Wretched is what your faith commands you, if you are believers.”","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The omitted conditional response","html":"<p>I say: “if you are” is a conditional clause whose response is omitted: if you are believers, wretched is what your faith commands you.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “if you are” is a conditional clause whose response is omitted: if you are believers, wretched is what your faith commands you."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Love fixed in the heart like dye in cloth","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember also when We bound you to act according to the Torah, but you refused. We raised the Mount above you and said: hold firmly and earnestly to what We have given you, and hear what I tell you in it. They said through the tongue of their state, by failing to comply, “We have heard Your speech and disobeyed Your command”; or they said it aloud, through bad comportment.<\/p><p>Their hearts drank in love of the calf until it coloured them and became fixed as dye becomes fixed in cloth. For they were corporealists (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجسّمة<\/bdi>) and had never seen a sight more wonderful than the calf fashioned by al-Sāmirī. Say to them, Muḥammad: wretched is what your alleged faith in the Torah commands you, if you are believers. Faith, however, does not command this, so you are not believers.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: remember also when We bound you to act according to the Torah, but you refused. We raised the Mount above you and said: hold firmly and earnestly to what We have given you, and hear what I tell you in it. They said through the tongue of their state, by failing to comply, “We have heard Your speech and disobeyed Your command”; or they said it aloud, through bad comportment. Their hearts drank in love of the calf until it coloured them and became fixed as dye becomes fixed in cloth. For they were corporealists ( مجسّمة ) and had never seen a sight more wonderful than the calf fashioned by al-Sāmirī. Say to them, Muḥammad: wretched is what your alleged faith in the Torah commands you, if you are believers. Faith, however, does not command this, so you are not believers."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The covenant to struggle against habitual patterns","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to someone claiming perfect faith while denying the people of spiritual excellence, persisting in his soul’s habits and veiled by witnessing sensible things: We took your covenant to struggle against your lower selves and break your habits, so that you might enter your Lord’s presence.<\/p><p>We raised above your heads the swords of warning or the mountains of longing, made the level way clear, and told you to take earnestly what We gave you of breaking habits and acquiring benefits. But you refused: your selves were too dear to you. Through the tongue of your state you said, “We hear and disobey.” Your hearts drank in love of the immediate world, and you preferred this world to the next. Wretched is what your faith commands you, if you are believers.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says to someone claiming perfect faith while denying the people of spiritual excellence, persisting in his soul’s habits and veiled by witnessing sensible things: We took your covenant to struggle against your lower selves and break your habits, so that you might enter your Lord’s presence. We raised above your heads the swords of warning or the mountains of longing, made the level way clear, and told you to take earnestly what We gave you of breaking habits and acquiring benefits. But you refused: your selves were too dear to you. Through the tongue of your state you said, “We hear and disobey.” Your hearts drank in love of the immediate world, and you preferred this world to the next. Wretched is what your faith commands you, if you are believers."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Among the Jews’ claims was that Paradise belonged exclusively to them. God refuted them, saying:<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Among the Jews’ claims was that Paradise belonged exclusively to them. God refuted them, saying:"}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1842,"arabic_characters":1499},{"id":"q2-94-95","q":"2:94–95","s":2,"surah":"Al-Baqara","title":"2:94–95 · Longing for the meeting with God","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 137–138","cairo":"I:137–138","source_page_first":137,"source_page_last":138,"ar":"قُلْ إِنْ كانَتْ لَكُمُ الدَّارُ الْآخِرَةُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ خالِصَةً مِنْ دُونِ النَّاسِ فَتَمَنَّوُا الْمَوْتَ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ (94) وَلَنْ يَتَمَنَّوْهُ أَبَداً بِما قَدَّمَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالظَّالِمِينَ (95)\nقلت: (خالصة) خبر كان، و (عند) متعلق بكان على الأصح.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: قُلْ يا محمد لبني إسرائيل الذين ادعوا أن الجنة خاصة بهم: إِنْ كانَتْ لَكُمُ الدَّارُ الْآخِرَةُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ أي: في غيبه، خالِصَةً لكم مِنْ دُونِ سائر النَّاسِ، أو من دون المسلمين، فَتَمَنَّوُا الْمَوْتَ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ في اختصاصكم بها، فإن العبد إذا تحقق أنه صائر إليها اشتاق إلى الموت الذي يوصل إليها، كما قال عمار رضي الله عنه عند موته:\nالآنَ ألاقِي الأحِبَّهْ ... مُحَمَّداً وحِزْبَه\nوقال حذيفة رضي الله عنه حين احتضر: (جَاءَ حَبِيبٌ عَلَى فَاقَة، لاَ أَفْلَحَ مَنْ نَدِم) . أي: على التمني، أو على الدنيا.\nقال تعالى: وَلَنْ يَتَمَنَّوْهُ أَبَداً بسبب بِما قَدَّمَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ من الكفر والعصيان، فما تمناه أحد منهم قط، قال ابن عباس: (لو أن اليهود تمنوا الموت لماتوا ورأوا مقاعدهم في النار) . وقال فى الإحياء:\n(دعا- عليه الصلاة والسلام- اليهود إلى تمني الموت، وأخبرهم بأنهم لا يتمنونه، فحيل بينهم وبين النطق بذلك) . وذكر غيره: أن بعضهم تمناه، فما جاءت العشاء حتى أخذته الدّبحة في حلقه فمات .\nوَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالظَّالِمِينَ، فيه تهديد لهم وتنبيه على أنهم ظالمون في دعوى ما ليس لهم، ونفيه عمن هو لهم.\nالإشارة: في هذه الآية ميزان صحيح توزن به الأعمال والأحوال ويتميز به المدعون من الأبطال، فكل عمل يهدمه الموت فهو مدخول، وكل حال يهزمه الموت فهو معلول، وكل من فرّ من الموت فهو في دعواه المحبة كذاب، فمن ادعى الخصوصية على الناس يختبر بهذه الآية.\nوالناس في حب البقاء في الدنيا على أربعة أقسام:\nرجل أحب البقاء في الدنيا لاغتنام لَذَّاته ونيل شهواته، قد طرح أخراه، وأكبَّ على دنياه، واتخذ إلهه هواه، فأصمه ذلك وأعماه، إن ذُكر له الموت فرّ عنه وشرد، وإن وعِظَ أنِف وعَنَدَ، عمره ينقص، وحرصه يزيد، وجسمه يبلى، وأمله جديد، وحتفه قريب، ومطلبه بعيد، فهذا إن لم تكن له عناية أزلية، وسابقة أولية فيمسك عليه الإيمان، ويختم له بالإسلام، وإلا فقد هلك.\nورجل قد أزيل عن عينه قذاها، وأبصر نفسه وهواها، وزجرها ونهاها، قد شمر ليتلافى ما فات، ونظر فيما هو آت، وتأهب لحلول الممات، والانتقال إلى محلة الأموات، ومع هذا فإنه يكره الموت أن يشاهد وقائعه، أو يرى طلائعه، وليس يكره الموت لذاته، ولا لأنه هَادِمْ لَذَّاتِهِ، لكنه يخاف أن يقطعه عن الاستعداد ليوم المعاد، ويكره أن تطوى صحيفةِ عَمَلِهِ قبل بلوغ أمله، وأن يبادر بأجله قبل صلاح خلله، فهو يريد البقاء في هذه الدار لقضاء هذه الأوطار، فهذا ما أفضل حياته: وأطيب مماته! لا يدخل تحت قوله صلّى الله عليه وسلّم: «مَنْ كَرِهَ لِقَاءَ اللهِ كره اللهُ لِقَاءَه» .\nورجل آخر قد عرف الله تعالى بأسمائه الحسنى، وصفاته العليا، وشهد ما شهد من كمال الربوبية، وجمال حضرة الألوهية، فملأت عينه وقلبه، وأطاشت عقله ولبّه، فهو يحن إلى ذلك المشهد، ويستعجل إنجاز ذلك الموعد، قد علم أن الحياة الدنيوية حجابٌ بينه وبين محبوبه، وسترٌ مُسدل بينه وبين مطلوبه، فهذا من المحبين العشاق، قد حنّ إلى الوصال والتلاق، أحب لقاء الله فأحب الله لقاءه، فما أحسن حياته ولقاءه! ورجل آخر قد شهد ما شاهد ذلك، وربما زاد على ما هنالك، لكنه فوّض الأمر إلى خالقه، وسلّم الأمر لبارئه، فلم يرض إلا ما رضي له، ولم يرد إلا ما أريد به، وما اختار إلا ما حكم به فيه، إن أبقاه في هذه الدار أبقاه، وإن أخذه فهو بغيته ومناه، فهذا من العارفين المقربين. جعلنا الله منهم بمنِّه وكرمه. آمين.\n\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى ما يبطل دعواهم أن الجنة خالصة لهم؛ وهو حرصهم على البقاء في هذه الدار، فقال:","quran_ar":"قُلْ إِنْ كانَتْ لَكُمُ الدَّارُ الْآخِرَةُ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ خالِصَةً مِنْ دُونِ النَّاسِ فَتَمَنَّوُا الْمَوْتَ إِنْ كُنْتُمْ صادِقِينَ (94) وَلَنْ يَتَمَنَّوْهُ أَبَداً بِما قَدَّمَتْ أَيْدِيهِمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِالظَّالِمِينَ (95)","quran_en":"94. Say, “If the abode of the Hereafter with God belongs exclusively to you, apart from other people, then wish for death, if you are truthful.” 95. But they will never wish for it, because of what their hands have sent ahead. God knows the wrongdoers.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Exclusivity and the predicate","html":"<p>I say: “exclusively” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">خالصة<\/bdi>) is the predicate of <i>kāna<\/i>. On the sounder view, “with” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عند<\/bdi>) is attached to <i>kāna<\/i>.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “exclusively” ( خالصة ) is the predicate of kāna . On the sounder view, “with” ( عند ) is attached to kāna ."},{"type":"exposition","title":"An exclusive claim put to the test","html":"<p>The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: tell the Children of Israel who claim that Paradise is theirs alone: if the abode of the Hereafter with God—that is, in His unseen knowledge—belongs exclusively to you, apart from everyone else or apart from the Muslims, wish for death, if your claim is true. For when a servant is certain that he is going to Paradise, he longs for the death that brings him there. As ʿAmmār, God be pleased with him, said when dying:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Now I meet the beloved ones:<br>Muḥammad and his company.<\/blockquote><p>Ḥudhayfa, God be pleased with him, said at the approach of death: “A beloved friend has come at a time of need. May whoever regrets it not prosper”—meaning regret for wishing for it, or regret over the world.<a class=\"source-marker\" href=\"Source_Reference_Ledger_XII.html#LEGACY-66\" title=\"Inherited reference occurrence; see current source status\">[B062]<\/a><\/p><p>God Most High says: they will never wish for it, because their hands have sent ahead unbelief and disobedience. None of them ever wished for it. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “Had the Jews wished for death, they would have died and seen their places in the Fire.” In the <i>Iḥyāʾ<\/i>: “He, peace and blessings be upon him, invited the Jews to wish for death and told them they would not wish for it: they were prevented from uttering it.” Another writer mentions that one of them did wish for it, and before nightfall a choking inflammation took him in the throat and he died.<\/p><p>“God knows the wrongdoers” threatens them and indicates their wrongdoing in claiming what is not theirs and denying it to those to whom it belongs.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"The Real, glorious is His majesty, says: tell the Children of Israel who claim that Paradise is theirs alone: if the abode of the Hereafter with God—that is, in His unseen knowledge—belongs exclusively to you, apart from everyone else or apart from the Muslims, wish for death, if your claim is true. For when a servant is certain that he is going to Paradise, he longs for the death that brings him there. As ʿAmmār, God be pleased with him, said when dying: Now I meet the beloved ones: Muḥammad and his company. Ḥudhayfa, God be pleased with him, said at the approach of death: “A beloved friend has come at a time of need. May whoever regrets it not prosper”—meaning regret for wishing for it, or regret over the world. [B062] God Most High says: they will never wish for it, because their hands have sent ahead unbelief and disobedience. None of them ever wished for it. Ibn ʿAbbās said: “Had the Jews wished for death, they would have died and seen their places in the Fire.” In the Iḥyāʾ : “He, peace and blessings be upon him, invited the Jews to wish for death and told them they would not wish for it: they were prevented from uttering it.” Another writer mentions that one of them did wish for it, and before nightfall a choking inflammation took him in the throat and he died. “God knows the wrongdoers” threatens them and indicates their wrongdoing in claiming what is not theirs and denying it to those to whom it belongs."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Four attitudes towards remaining in this world","html":"<p>This verse contains a sound balance in which deeds and states are weighed and pretenders distinguished from true champions. Every deed that death demolishes is adulterated; every state that death defeats is defective. Whoever flees death lies in claiming love. A person claiming special election above others is tested by this verse.<\/p><p>People fall into four classes in their love of remaining in this world. One loves to remain in order to seize its pleasures and satisfy his desires. He casts aside his Hereafter, bends over his world and takes his desire as his god; this deafens and blinds him. Mention death and he flees; admonish him and he takes offence and resists. His lifespan diminishes while his avidity grows; his body wears out while his expectations renew themselves. His death is near, his aspirations remote. Unless eternal care and an antecedent gift preserve his faith and seal his life with Islam, he is lost. [Translator’s note: Cairo I:138 prints إن لم تكن له عناية أزلية ... فيمسك عليه الإيمان ... وإلا فقد هلك: “if there is no eternal care,” followed by preservation of faith and “otherwise he has perished.” The corresponding passage in ʿAbd al-Ḥaqq al-Ishbīlī’s <i>al-ʿĀqiba<\/i> (ed. Khiḍr Muḥammad Khiḍr, 1406/1986, p. 29) has the same difficult negative conditional, with dual pronouns for its preceding two classes. A public manuscript facsimile of that earlier work (Wikisource file <i>al-ʿĀqiba fī aḥwāl al-ākhira kh.<\/i>, PDF image 7, visible folio numeral 4) omits this entire conditional and the following supplication. It supplies no variant particle and does not settle the difficulty. The English expresses the contextual warning; the exact syntax and authorial wording remain unresolved.]<\/p><p>Another has had the mote removed from his eye: he perceives his soul and its desires, restrains and forbids it, girds himself to make good what has passed, considers what is coming, and prepares for death and departure to the dwelling of the dead. Yet he dislikes witnessing death’s events or seeing its advance guard. He dislikes it neither for its own sake nor because it destroys his pleasures, but fears being cut off from preparation for the Day of Return. He dislikes having his record of deeds rolled up before attaining his hope, or his term arriving before he has put his defects right. He wishes to remain here to fulfil these purposes. How excellent his life, how good his death! He is not included in the saying, “Whoever dislikes meeting God, God dislikes meeting him.”<\/p><p>A third knows God through His most beautiful Names and highest Attributes. He has witnessed something of the perfection of Lordship and the beauty of the presence of Divinity; it fills his eyes and heart and carries away his reason and understanding. He yearns for that vision and hastens towards fulfilment of that promise. He knows that worldly life is a veil between him and his Beloved, a hanging curtain between him and what he seeks. Such a person is among the passionate lovers, longing for union and encounter. He loves meeting God, and God loves meeting him. How beautiful his life and his meeting!<\/p><p>A fourth has witnessed what the third witnessed, perhaps more. But he entrusts the matter to his Creator and surrenders it to his Maker. He approves only what is approved for him, desires only what is desired for him, and chooses only what is decreed concerning him. If God keeps him here, He keeps him; if He takes him, that is his desire and hope. He is among the gnostics brought near. May God make us among them through His favour and generosity. Amen.<\/p><p>The Real Most High then mentions what refutes their claim that the Garden belongs exclusively to them: their eagerness to remain in this abode. He says:<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"This verse contains a sound balance in which deeds and states are weighed and pretenders distinguished from true champions. Every deed that death demolishes is adulterated; every state that death defeats is defective. Whoever flees death lies in claiming love. A person claiming special election above others is tested by this verse. People fall into four classes in their love of remaining in this world. One loves to remain in order to seize its pleasures and satisfy his desires. He casts aside his Hereafter, bends over his world and takes his desire as his god; this deafens and blinds him. Mention death and he flees; admonish him and he takes offence and resists. His lifespan diminishes while his avidity grows; his body wears out while his expectations renew themselves. His death is near, his aspirations remote. Unless eternal care and an antecedent gift preserve his faith and seal his life with Islam, he is lost. [Translator’s note: Cairo I:138 prints إن لم تكن له عناية أزلية ... فيمسك عليه الإيمان ... وإلا فقد هلك: “if there is no eternal care,” followed by preservation of faith and “otherwise he has perished.” The corresponding passage in ʿAbd al-Ḥaqq al-Ishbīlī’s al-ʿĀqiba (ed. Khiḍr Muḥammad Khiḍr, 1406/1986, p. 29) has the same difficult negative conditional, with dual pronouns for its preceding two classes. A public manuscript facsimile of that earlier work (Wikisource file al-ʿĀqiba fī aḥwāl al-ākhira kh. , PDF image 7, visible folio numeral 4) omits this entire conditional and the following supplication. It supplies no variant particle and does not settle the difficulty. The English expresses the contextual warning; the exact syntax and authorial wording remain unresolved.] Another has had the mote removed from his eye: he perceives his soul and its desires, restrains and forbids it, girds himself to make good what has passed, considers what is coming, and prepares for death and departure to the dwelling of the dead. Yet he dislikes witnessing death’s events or seeing its advance guard. He dislikes it neither for its own sake nor because it destroys his pleasures, but fears being cut off from preparation for the Day of Return. He dislikes having his record of deeds rolled up before attaining his hope, or his term arriving before he has put his defects right. He wishes to remain here to fulfil these purposes. How excellent his life, how good his death! He is not included in the saying, “Whoever dislikes meeting God, God dislikes meeting him.” A third knows God through His most beautiful Names and highest Attributes. He has witnessed something of the perfection of Lordship and the beauty of the presence of Divinity; it fills his eyes and heart and carries away his reason and understanding. He yearns for that vision and hastens towards fulfilment of that promise. He knows that worldly life is a veil between him and his Beloved, a hanging curtain between him and what he seeks. Such a person is among the passionate lovers, longing for union and encounter. He loves meeting God, and God loves meeting him. How beautiful his life and his meeting! A fourth has witnessed what the third witnessed, perhaps more. But he entrusts the matter to his Creator and surrenders it to his Maker. He approves only what is approved for him, desires only what is desired for him, and chooses only what is decreed concerning him. If God keeps him here, He keeps him; if He takes him, that is his desire and hope. He is among the gnostics brought near. May God make us among them through His favour and generosity. Amen. The Real Most High then mentions what refutes their claim that the Garden belongs exclusively to them: their eagerness to remain in this abode. He says:"}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C137.1","page":137,"ar":"لم أقف على ما يفيد ذلك؛ ولو وقع لنقل واشتهر لتوافر الدواعي إلى نقله؛ لأنه أمر عظيم. بل على العكس؛ فالأخبار الواردة في أنهم ما تمنوا بلغت مبلغ التواتر، كما يقول الفخر الرازي.","en":"I have found nothing establishing this. Had it happened, it would have been transmitted and become widely known, since the motives for transmitting so momentous an event would have been abundant. On the contrary, the reports that they did not wish for death attain the level of mass transmission, as Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī says.","anchor":"أن بعضهم تمناه ... فمات"}],"english_characters":5262,"arabic_characters":3276}];
